\id 2SA - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.20
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Samuel
\toc1 2 Shmuel
\toc2 2 Samuel
\mt1 2 Shmuel
\mt2 (2 Samuel)
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 David learns about Saul’s death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 After David returned from slaughtering the Amalekites, he stayed in Tsiklag for two days. (\add King\add* Saul was dead by this time.)
\v 2 Then on the third day, wow, a man came from Saul’s camp. He had torn clothes and dirt on his head, and when he got to David he fell onto his knees and bowed low.
\v 3 “Where have you come from?” David asked him.
\p “I was in Israel’s camp, but managed to escape.” he replied.
\v 4 “Why? What happened?” David demanded. “Tell me, please,”
\p “Our people fled from the battle,” the man said. “Many fell and died. Even Saul and his son Jonathan died.”
\p
\v 5 “How do you know that Saul and Jonathan died?” David asked the young man who’d brought the news.
\p
\v 6 “I happened to be on Mt. Gilboa,” the young man replied, “and listen, Saul was leaning on his spear. Then wow, the chariots and the masters of the chariot horses overtook him,
\v 7 and he turned around and saw me. He called me and I answered, ‘Here I am.’
\v 8 ‘Who are you?’ he asked. ‘I’m an Amalekite,’ I told him.
\v 9 Then he said to me, ‘Please, come over here and kill me, because although I’m still alive, the pain is unbearable.’
\v 10 So I stood over him and killed him, because I knew that with those wounds, he wouldn’t live. Then I took the crown off his head and the bracelet on his arm, and I brought them here to you, my master.”
\p
\v 11 Then David pulled at his clothes \add with his hands\add* and tore them, and all the men with him did the same,
\v 12 and they mourned and wept and fasted until the evening for Saul and for his son Jonathan, and for Yahweh’s people, because \add many\add* Israelites had been killed.
\p
\v 13 Then David asked the young man who’d brought the news, “Where are you from?”
\p “I’m a foreigner’s son,” he replied, “an Amalekite.”
\v 14 “How come you weren’t afraid to kill Yahweh’s anointed \add king\add* with your own hands?” David asked him.
\v 15 Then David called to one of his young men, “Come over here \add and\add* execute him.” So he struck him, and he died.
\v 16 “Your blood’s on your \add own\add* head,” David told the Amalekite, “because you admitted in your own words that you, yourself killed Yahweh’s anointed \add one\add*.”
\s1 David’s song of mourning
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 17 Then David sang this funeral song for Saul and his son Jonathan
\v 18 and said \add that it should be\add* taught to the people of Judah. \add It’s called\add* ‘The bow’ and indeed, it’s written \add down\add* in the Book of Yashar:
\q1
\v 19 “Israel’s splendour \add was\add* slain in the hills.
\q1 How the powerful \add warriors\add* have died.
\q1
\v 20 Don’t tell them in Gath.
\q1 Don’t let them take the news to Ashkelon’s streets,
\q1 in case the Philistine women celebrate,
\q1 and the daughters of the uncircumcised are elated.
\b
\q1
\v 21 You mountains in Gilboa,
\q2 \add may you have\add* no dew or rain \add fall\add* on you,
\q2 nor fields \add producing grain\add* for offerings.
\q1 Because it was there that \add Saul’s\add* shield was splattered with his blood,
\q2 and \add the leather\add* won’t be preserved with oil \add again\add*.
\q1
\v 22 From the blood of those slain—
\q2 from the fat of the warriors,
\q1 Jonathan’s bow didn’t retreat
\q2 and Saul’s sword didn’t fail on its strikes.
\b
\q1
\v 23 Saul and Jonathan were loved—
\q2 they pleased the people.
\q2 and even at their death they weren’t separated.
\q1 They were swifter than eagles.
\q2 they were stronger than lions.
\b
\q1
\v 24 Weep for Saul you daughters of Israel,
\q1 the one who dressed you in nice clothes with jewellery,
\q1 and gave you all gold brooches to put on.
\b
\q1
\v 25 Those warriors have fallen in the middle of the battle.
\q1 Jonathan has been killed \add there\add* on the hills.
\b
\q1
\v 26 I grieve for you my dear friend Jonathan.
\q1 You were so kind to me.
\q1 Your friendship meant more to me than the women who say they love me.
\b
\q1
\v 27 \add Yes, those\add* powerful warriors have fallen,
\q1 and those weapons of war have been destroyed.”
\c 2
\s1 David’s kingdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 After that was over, David inquired from Yahweh, “Should I go into one of Judah’s cities?”
\p “\add Yes\add*, go,” Yahweh answered.
\p “Where should I go?” David asked \add again\add*.
\p “To Hebron,” he said.
\p
\v 2 So David took his two wives (Ahinoam the Yizre’eli and Abigail the widow of Naval the Karmeli) and went there.
\v 3 He took his men who were with him, along with their families, and went to live in Hebron and the \add surrounding\add* towns.
\v 4 Then the Judah leaders came and anointed David as king over Judah.
\p They told him, “It was the men of Yavesh-Gilead who buried Saul.”
\v 5 So David sent messengers to Yavesh-Gilead to tell them, “May Yahweh bless you all because you showed that kindness to your master Saul to bury him \add respectfully\add*.
\v 6 Now may Yahweh treat you all with kindness and faithfulness, and I myself will also be good to you because you did that.
\v 7 Meanwhile now that Saul your master is dead, \add continue\add* to be courageous and become brave warriors as Judah has anointed me as king over their tribe.
\s1 Disputes between Saul and David’s families
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 8 \add However,\add* Abner (Ner’s son) who’d been Saul’s army commander had taken Saul’s son Ish-Boshet\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Also known as Esh-Baal. (See 1 Chronicles 8:33.)\f* across to Mahanayim
\v 9 and declared him to be king over Gilead, and over the Ashurim and Jezreel, and over Ephraim and Benjamin, and over all of Israel.
\v 10 Ish-Boshet was forty years old when he started to rule over the Israelites, and he ruled them for two years.
\p But the Judah tribe was loyal to David
\v 11 and he ruled them for seven and a half years.
\p
\v 12 \add One day\add* Abner left Mahanayim with \add some of\add* Ish-Boshet’s servants and went to Gibeon,
\v 13 but Joab Tseruyah’s son, \add traditionally called ‘Joab’ in English\add*) \add took some of\add* David’s servants and went and confronted them at the Gibeon pool—one group on each side of the pool.
\v 14 Then Abner said to Joab, “Let’s get some of these young warriors to fight it out in front of us.”
\p “Sounds good to me,” said Joab.
\p
\v 15 So they stood up and counted off twelve representing Benjamin and Saul’s son Ish-Boshet and twelve from David’s servants.
\v 16 Each of them grabbed his opponent’s head and then thrust his sword into his side, so they both fell \add down\add* dead together. So that place in Gibeon was named ‘Helkat-Hatsurim’ \add (meaning ‘Field of Daggers’)\add*.
\p
\v 17 \add That led on to\add* a very intense battle that day, but Abner and the Israelite men were defeated by David’s servants.
\v 18 Tseruyah’s three sons were there: Joab, Avishay, and Asahel, (Asahel was a fast runner—like a wild antelope.)
\v 19 and Asahel chased after Abner and wouldn’t give up.
\v 20 Abner turned and said, “Is that you, Asahel?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* it’s me,” he answered.
\p
\v 21 “\add I’m warning you:\add* Stop chasing me and go after someone else instead,” Abner called back. But Asahel wouldn’t give up
\v 22 so Abner tried again, “Stop chasing me! You’ll force me to kill you, then how could I face your brother Joab?”
\v 23 But Asahel wouldn’t give up so Abner thrust the butt end of his spear into his stomach and it went right through and came out his back. He fell to the ground and died there, and when the others caught up, they stopped there.
\p
\v 24 But Joab and Avishay kept going after Abner. As the sun was setting, they came to the Amah hill (east of Giyah, along the road to the wilderness near Gibeon)
\v 25 and the Benyamites lined up behind Abner as an coordinated unit there on top of the hill.
\v 26 Abner called out to Joab, “Are we just going to keep killing each other? Don’t you know that the only result can be more bitterness? How long will you go on before you command your people to stop killing their cousins?”
\p
\v 27 “As God lives,” Joab replied, “if you hadn’t said that, then surely it would have been morning before they would have pulled back from the chase.”
\v 28 So Joab had a trumpet blown and his men stopped fighting and pursing the Israelites.
\p
\v 29 Then Abner and his men walked all that night through the Jordan river plain, then crossed the river and walked all morning until they got \add back\add* to Mahanayim.
\p
\v 30 When Joab had assembled them all \add and counted them, only\add* Asahel and nineteen others had been killed in the battle,
\v 31 although they had killed 360 of Abner’s Benyamite warriors.
\v 32 They retrieved Asahel’s body and buried it in his father’s tomb in Bethlehem. Then they walked all night and got \add back\add* to Hebron at first light.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Nevertheless the war between Saul’s and David’s followers dragged on and on, but \add overall,\add* David’s side had more wins than the followers of Ish-Boshet.
\s1 David’s sons
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 2 David’s wives gave birth to \add six\add* sons \add while they were based there\add* in Hebron: Ahinoam (the Yizre’eli) gave birth to Amnon,
\v 3 then Abigail (the widow of Naval the Karmeli) gave birth to Kilav, and Ma’akah (daughter of King Talmay of Geshur) gave birth to Absalom.
\v 4 The fourth was Adonijah (son of Hagit), the fifth was Shefatyah (son of Avital),
\v 5 and the sixth was Yitream (son of David’s wife Eglah). \add All six of\add* those sons were born to David in Hebron.
\s1 Abner switches over to David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 While the war between Saul’s son Ish-Boshet and David’s supporters continued, Abner was strengthening himself with Saul’s supporters.
\v 7 Now Saul had had a slave-wife named Ritspah (daughter of Ayah) and Abner slept with her. So Ish-Boshet demanded, “Why have you slept with my father’s slave-wife?”
\v 8 Abner became very angry at that challenge from Ish-Boshet and snapped back, “Do you think I’m Judah’s dog \add hiding out over here\add*? Today I’ve been helping Saul’s family: your father, his brothers, and his friends, and I won’t let you fall into David’s hands. Yet today you bring some charge against me to do with a woman?
\v 9 May God punish me if I don’t do for David exactly what Yahweh promised him
\v 10 \add and help with\add* the transfer of the kingdom away from your family and to establish David’s kingdom over \add both\add* Judah and Israel, from Dan \add down\add* to Beersheba.”
\v 11 Ish-Boshet didn’t say a word back to Abner because he was \add very\add* scared of him.
\p
\v 12 Then Abner sent messengers to David to tell him, “Who should be king of this country? Let’s come to an agreement and then I could join you \add and help\add* make all of Israel turn to you.”
\p
\v 13 “\add Yes,\add* good,” David replied. “I’m happy to come to an agreement with you on one condition: you must bring my wife—Saul’s daughter, Michal—if you want to be accepted here.”
\v 14 Then David sent messengers to Saul’s son Ish-Boshet saying, “Give Michal back to me. I earnt her as my wife with the foreskins of one-hundred Philistines.”
\v 15 So Ish-Boshet had her taken from her husband (Layish’s son Paltiel),
\v 16 but he walked along behind her, weeping, as far as Bahurim \add where finally\add* Abner told him to go back home and so he did.
\p
\v 17 Then Abner conferred with the Israelite elders saying, “Previously you were wanting David to be king over you,
\v 18 but now it’s time for action. Yahweh has \add already\add* said, ‘I’m going to use my servant David to rescue my people Israel from the Philistines and all their \add other\add* enemies.’ ”
\v 19 Abner also spoke privately with the Benyamites, then he went to Hebron to tell David privately what the Israelites and the Benyamites had agreed to.
\p
\v 20 Then Abner took twenty men and went to David at Hebron, and David put on a feast for all of them.
\v 21 Abner asked David, “My master, the king. Let me get ready and go, and let me encourage all of Israel that they’ll make an agreement with you so that you will be king over the entire area as you’ve been wanting.” So David sent Abner, and he went in peace.
\s1 Joab murders Abner
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 22 \add Soon afterwards,\add* Joab and some of David’s men arrived after a raid and carried in a lot of plunder. (Abner had already left Hebron because David had sent him off and he’d left in peace.)
\v 23 When Joab and all the fighters with him arrived, he was told that Abner had been there and spoken with the king, then sent away in peace.
\v 24 So Joab went to the king and demanded, “What have you done? Abner came to you and I can’t believe that you’d let him go, but he’s definitely not here now.
\v 25 Surely you realise that he \add only\add* came here to influence you and to find out where you’re coming from and going to, and to learn everything that you’re doing?”
\p
\v 26 Once Joab had finished talking to David, he sent messengers to catch up to Abner. They \add found him\add* at the Sirah cistern and brought him back without David’s knowledge.
\v 27 When Abner got back to Hebron, Joab took him aside at the \add city\add* gate as if to speak to him privately, then stabbed him in the stomach and he died as vengeance for killing Joab’s brother Asahel.
\v 28 \add Later\add* when David heard about it, he declared, “I and my kingdom are innocent of Abner’s death in Yahweh’s eyes.
\v 29 May Joab’s extended family continually suffer from sores or leprosy, and be forced into manual labour or be killed in battle, or run out of food to eat.”
\v 30 (\add That’s how\add* Joab and his brother Avishay murdered Abner because he’d killed their brother Asahel in Gibeon during the war.)
\s1 Abnir’s burial
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 31 Then David instructed Joab and all the people with him, “Tear your clothes and put on \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth and mourn for Abner.” \add Later at the funeral,\add* King David walked behind the coffin
\v 32 and they buried Abner in Hebron. The king cried loudly at the tomb, and the people \add also\add* wept.
\v 33 The king sang this song for Abner:
\q1 “Abner shouldn’t have died as if he was just some drunken fool.
\q1
\v 34 He wasn’t handcuffed or with his feet in chains.
\q1 He fell like someone disturbing a burglar.”
\m And \add so\add* the people wept even more for him.
\p
\v 35 Many people brought food to David during the day, but he promised, “May God severely punish me if I eat anything before the sun goes down.”
\v 36 All the people recognised \add David’s dignity in handling that matter and\add* were pleased \add with his behaviour, just as they\add* were pleased \add with everything that the king did\add*,
\v 37 and everyone throughout Israel knew that the king hadn’t ordered for Abner (Ner’s son) to be executed.
\v 38 Then the king told his servants, “Don’t you all realise that Israel lost a leader and a great man today?
\v 39 Although I was anointed as king, I feel tender today. Those sons of Tseruyah are more violent than me. May Yahweh repay evil people appropriately for their actions.”
\c 4
\s1 The murder of Ish-Boshet
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 When Saul’s son \add Ish-Boshet\add* heard that Abner had been killed in Hebron, he lost courage and all Israel was horrified.
\v 2 He had two men who were leaders of raiding parties. They were Rimon’s sons Ba’anah and Rekav from Be’erot in the Benyamite region,
\v 3 but the people from Be’erot had fled to Gitayim where they’ve stayed until now.
\p
\v 4 (Saul’s son Jonathan had a son named Mephibosheth. He was five years old when the report about Saul and Jonathan’s \add death\add* came from Jezreel. Mephibosheth’s nanny picked him up to run to safety, but in her hurry he fell and became crippled in his legs.)
\p
\v 5 \add One day,\add* Ba’anah and Rekav (the sons of Rimon from Be’erot) walked to Ish-Boshet’s house, arriving in the middle of the day when he was taking a nap.
\v 6-7 They entered the middle of the house as if getting wheat, then \add entered\add* the inner room where @Ish-Boshet was on the bed. They struck him in the body, killing him, then they cut off his head. Then Rekav and Ba’anah took the head and escaped, and walked all night through the Aravah.
\v 8 They took Ish-Boshet’s head to David at Hebron and told the king, “Look, \add here’s\add* the head of Ish-Boshet, the son of your enemy Saul who tried to kill you. Today Yahweh’s given vengeance to my master the king against Saul and his descendants.”
\p
\v 9 Then David answered Rekav and his brother Ba’anah (the sons of Rimon from Be’erot), “As Yahweh lives, having delivered my life from every distress,
\v 10 when the messenger came to Tsiklag thinking he was bringing me good news and told me that Saul was dead, I had him seized and executed.
\v 11 \add Now\add* how much more when wicked men have killed an innocent man on his bed in his \add own\add* house. Shouldn’t I avenge you \add two\add* for his murder and remove you from the earth?”
\v 12 Then David commanded \add some of\add* the young men and they killed the two brothers and cut of their hands and feet and hung \add them\add*\f + \fr 4:12 \ft It’s ambiguous in the Hebrew whether the hands and feet were strung up, or the actual bodies.\f* beside the Hebron pool, but they took Ish-Boshet’s head and buried it \add there\add* in Hebron in Abner’s tomb.
\c 5
\s1 David becomes king of all Israel
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 11:1-9; 14:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Then \add the leaders of\add* all of Israel’s tribes came to David at Hebron and said to him, “Listen, we’re \add all\add* close relations.
\v 2 In the past when Saul was our king, you were the one who actually showed leadership of the people. Yahweh has \add already\add* told you that you will shepherd Israel and become our king.”
\v 3 So with Yahweh as their witness, all the Israelite elders made a formal agreement with David, and they anointed him as king over \add all\add* Israel.
\v 4 David was thirty years old when he became their king and he reigned for \add a total of\add* forty years:
\v 5 seven and a half years in Hebron as king of Judah, then thirty-three years in Jerusalem as king over all Israel and Judah.
\s1 David captures Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 The king and his men \add battled\add* against the Jebusites who lived in and around Jerusalem, but Jebusites mocked David, “You’ll never break in here—even blind and crippled men could keep you out,” because they thought that David couldn’t get in.
\v 7 But David \em did\em* capture that Zion fortress that \add then became known as\add* David’s city.
\p
\v 8 He’d told his men that day, “Anyone \add wanting to\add* strike the Jebusites should use the water tunnel to reach the lame people and the blind people—the ones who are hated by David.” (That’s why they say that blind and crippled people shouldn’t enter the house.\f + \fr 5:8 \ft Left ambiguous here because it’s not clear from the Hebrew. (Some think ‘David’s palace’ while others go for ‘God’s temple’.)\f*)
\p
\v 9 So David lived in that fortress and called it ‘David’s city’—expanding it on all sides.
\v 10 David continued to grow more powerful because Yahweh, Commander-in-chief God was with him.
\p
\v 11 King Hiram from Tyre sent messengers to David, then he sent carpenters and stone-cutters to build a palace for David.
\p
\v 12 David knew that Yahweh had established him as king over \add all\add* Israel and that he was prospering the kingdom for the sake of his people the Israelites.
\p
\v 13 After David moved from Hebron to Jerusalem, he married more wives and took more slave-wives, all of whom produced more sons and daughters for him.
\v 14 The names of his sons who were born in Jerusalem were Shamua, Shovav, Nathan, Solomon,
\v 15 Yavehar, Elishua, Nefeg, Yafia,
\v 16 Elishama, Elyada, and Elifelet.
\s1 Defeated by David the Philistines
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 14:8-17)
\p
\v 17 When the Philistines heard that David had been declared as king over \add all\add* Israel, their army set off to capture him, but David heard and retreated\f + \fr 5:17 \ft The Hebrew says ‘descended’, but doesn’t tell us either from where (probably Jerusalem) or to where (possibly Masada).\f* to the fortress.
\v 18 The Philistines moved in and took over the Rafa valley,
\v 19 and David inquired from Yahweh, “Should I attack the Philistines? Will you help me defeat them?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* attack them,” Yahweh replied, “because I’ll certainly help you to defeat them.”
\p
\v 20 So David attacked and defeated them, and said, “Yahweh broke through my enemies in front of me like breaking a dam.” So he called the place ‘Baal-Peratsim’ \add (which means ‘Master of breaking through’)\add*.
\v 21 The Philistines left their idols there, and David and his men took them away.
\p
\v 22 Then the Philistines returned to the Rafa valley and took over it again.
\v 23 \add Again\add* David inquired from Yahweh who replied, “\add No,\add* don’t go straight in. Move around behind them and then attack them from opposite the trees.
\v 24 Then when you hear a sound in the tops of the trees that sounds like marching, then you’ll know that Yahweh has gone ahead to strike the Philistines’ camp.”
\v 25 So David followed Yahweh’s instructions they attacked the Philistines from Geva all the way to Gezer.
\c 6
\s1 The Box with the agreement is brought to Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 13:1-14; 15:25–16:6,43)
\p
\v 1 Then David selected thirty thousand Israelite warriors and gathered them together.
\v 2 He led them to \add Kiryat-Yearim (formerly called\add* Ba’alah) in Judah to get the box of God (whose name is Commander-in-chief Yahweh) who lives between the two winged creatures on the top of it.
\v 3 They placed the sacred chest on a newly-made cart and started moving it from Avinadav’s house which was on a hill. Uza and Ahyo (Avinadav’s sons) were leading the cart.
\v 4 They took it from Avinadav’s house and Ahyo took the lead in front.
\v 5 David and all the Israelites were celebrating in God’s presence with wooden harps and lyres, along with tambourines, shakers, and cymbals.
\p
\v 6 But when they reached Nakon’s threshing floor the oxen stumbled so Uza reached out to steady the sacred chest.
\v 7 Yahweh became furious with Uza and killed him \add right\add* there by the sacred chest because he’d touched it.
\v 8 \add Now\add* David got angry because of Yahweh’s outburst against Uza, and that place has been called Perez-Uza \add (meaning ‘The punishment of Uza’)\add* until today.
\v 9 David was afraid of Yahweh that day and asked, “How will Yahweh’s box get to Jerusalem?”
\v 10 So he decided not to take Yahweh’s box there and redirected it \add instead\add* to the house of Obed-Edom (a Giti).
\v 11 The sacred chest stayed at Obed-Edom’s house for three months, and Yahweh blessed him and all the household.
\p
\v 12 Someone told King David, “Yahweh has blessed Obed-Edom’s household because of the sacred chest,” so David went and brought God’s Box from Obed-Edom’s house to Jerusalem with much happiness.
\v 13 When the men who were carrying the sacred chest had walked six steps, they stopped and David sacrificed a bull and a fattened calf \add there\add*.
\v 14 Then David, wearing a linen apron, danced in front of Yahweh, putting everything into it.
\v 15 David and all the Israelites brought the sacred chest \add into Jerusalem\add* with shouting and trumpet blasts.
\p
\v 16 However, as they were entering the city, David’s wife Michal (Saul’s daughter) looked out the window and saw King David leaping and dancing in front of Yahweh, and she felt only despite for him.
\v 17 They took Yahweh’s box and placed it in the middle of the tent that David had erected for it. Then David offered burnt sacrifices to Yahweh, as well as peace offerings.
\v 18 When David had finished offering those sacrifices, he blessed the people in Commander-in-chief Yahweh’s name.
\v 19 He handed out a large bread roll, a cake of \add pressed\add* dates, and a cake of \add pressed\add* raisins to each man and woman \add there\add*, then they all returned to their homes.
\p
\v 20 Then David returned to bless his \add own\add* house, but his wife Michal came out to meet him complaining, “What disgraceful behaviour for Israel’s king today—dancing when he was wearing so little and exposing himself to his female servants like some brainless person!”
\p
\v 21 “It was to thank Yahweh,” David responded, “who chose me (rather than your father and any of his family) by appointing me as leader over Yahweh’s people Israel. I was celebrating in front of Yahweh
\v 22 and I will humiliate and humble myself \add even\add* more than that. Yet I will \add end up\add* being honoured by those female servants that you were talking about.”
\p
\v 23 \add After that,\add* Saul’s daughter Michal never had any more children.
\c 7
\s1 Yahweh’s promise to David
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 17:1-15)
\p
\v 1 Then the king moved into his palace, and Yahweh allowed him to have a time of peace from all his enemies.
\v 2 \add One day\add* the king said to the prophet Nathan, “Look, I’m \add here\add* living in a house \add made\add* of cedar, but God’s box is still there in a tent.”
\p
\v 3 “Go and do whatever you consider best,” Nathan replied, “because Yahweh is with you.”
\v 4 But that night, Yahweh spoke to Nathan,
\v 5 “Go and tell my servant David that Yahweh says, ‘Will you build a house for me to live in?
\v 6 I haven’t lived in a house since I brought the Israelites out of Egypt right up until now, \add but rather\add* I was living in a tent as they moved around.
\v 7 In all those places, I never once queried the leaders to ask why they never built a cedar house for me.’
\p
\v 8 “But now you should tell my servant David that Commander-in-chief Yahweh says, ‘I myself took you out of the grass field where you looked after the sheep and goats to become the leader of my people the Israelites.
\v 9 Everywhere you’ve gone, I’ve been with you and destroyed your enemies in front of you, and I will make you famous like all the famous people on the earth.
\v 10 I will establish a place for the Israelites and put them there to live, and they won’t be afraid because evil people won’t continue to oppress them like happened in the past
\v 11 when I appointed heroes \add (traditionally ‘judges’)\add* over my people Israel. I will give you peace from all your enemies, and I, Yahweh, will enable your descendants to rule after you.
\v 12 when your time on earth comes to an end and you’re laid down with your ancestors, I will appoint one of your sons to be king and I will establish his kingdom.
\v 13 He’s the one who’ll build a temple for me, and I will make his descendents reign forever.
\v 14 I myself will become like a father to him, and he’ll become like a son to me. When he disobeys, I’ll punish him like fathers punish their sons.
\v 15 But my kindness won’t turn away from you like I turned away from Saul—the one who I removed ahead of you.
\v 16 Your descendants and your kingdom will remain forever. Your throne will be established forever.’ ”
\s1 David’s prayer
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 17:16-27)
\p
\v 17 So Nathan passed all of that onto David,
\v 18 and King David went and sat in front of Yahweh and said, “Who am I, my master Yahweh, and how important is my family that you have brought me to this point?
\v 19 But that was just minor to you, my master Yahweh. You also spoke about the distant future for my descendants.
\v 20 What more can \add I,\add* David say to you? But my master Yahweh, you yourself know your servant.
\v 21 You’ve done all these incredible things in order to fulfil what you said and to fulfil your own desires, and then revealed them to your servant.
\v 22 That’s why you’re incredible, Yahweh my god, and so there’s no one like you. There’s no god except you—that’s what we keep hearing about.
\v 23 What other nation is like your people, Israel—one nation on the earth that you went to redeem for yourself as a people—rescuing you from Egypt and its gods? \add You\add* made a reputation for yourself and did amazing and scary things for your nation in front of your people
\v 24 You established \add us\add* Israelites as your \add own\add* people forever, and you yourself Yahweh became our God.
\p
\v 25 “And now, Yahweh God, the promise you made concerning your servant and his descendants, and do just as you have spoken and make it come true into the future.
\v 26 May your reputation be good forever, \add with people\add* saying, ‘Commander Yahweh is God over Israel,’ and the dynasty of your servant David will be established under you,
\v 27 because you, Commander-in-chief Yahweh are Israel’s god. You have told your servant, ‘I will build a dynasty for you.’ That’s why your servant has found the courage to pray this prayer.
\p
\v 28 “So now, my master Yahweh, you are God, and you do what you say, and you have said these good things concerning your servant.
\v 29 Now, if it pleases you, bless your servant’s extended family forever, because you yourself, my master Yahweh, have spoken, and from your blessing, your servant’s family will be blessed forever.”
\c 8
\s1 David’s victories
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 18:1-17)
\p
\v 1 Sometime later, David attacked the Philistines and defeated them, and he captured Meteg-Ha’amah from them.
\p
\v 2 Then he defeated the Moabites. David forced them to lie on the ground and used a length of rope to decide their fate—those inside two lengths of the rope were killed, and those inside the third length were spared and \add so\add* the Moabites became David’s servants—bringing him tribute.
\p
\v 3 Then David defeated Tsovah’s King Hadadezer (son of Rehov) who had tried to reestablish his authority over the Euphrates river.
\v 4 David captured 1,700 horsemen and twenty thousand men on foot. He hamstrung all the chariot \add horses\add* except for a hundred of them.
\p
\v 5 Then the Arameans came from Damascus to help Tsovah’s king Hadadezer but David killed twenty-two thousand of them.
\v 6 David stationed garrisons in Syria in Damascus) and the Arameans became David’s servants—bringing him tribute, and Yahweh helped David \add win\add* wherever he went.
\v 7 David confiscated the gold shields that Hadadezer’s servants carried and brought them to Jerusalem.
\v 8 He also brought a large amount of bronze out of Hadadezer’s cities of Betah and Berotah.
\p
\v 9 When Hamat’s King Toeu heard that David had defeated Hadadezer’s entire army,
\v 10 he sent his son Joram to ask for peace \add for Hamat\add* and to congratulate him for fighting Hadadezer and defeating him because Hadadezer often battled against Toeu. Joram brought containers with him, made of gold, silver, and bronze.
\v 11 King David dedicated them to Yahweh, \add along\add* with the silver and gold \add that he’d taken\add* from all the nations that they’d conquered:
\v 12 from Aram and Moab, from the Ammonites and the Philistines, and from Amalek and from the plunder of Tsovah’s King Hadadezer (son of Rehov).
\p
\v 13 Then David made \add more of\add* a name \add for himself\add* when he returned from defeating eighteen thousand Arameans in the Salt valley.
\v 14 He stationed garrisons throughout Edom and \add made the people\add* his servants, and Yahweh protected David wherever they fought.
\p
\v 15 So David reigned over all Israel and led all his people with justice and integrity.
\v 16 Tseruyah’s son Joab was the army commander and Ahilud’s son Jehoshaphat was the secretary.
\v 17 The priests were Ahituv’s son Zadok and Abiathar’s son Ahimelek, and Serayah was secretary.
\v 18 Yehoyada’s son Benayah \add supervised\add* the Keretim and the Peletim \add (David’s bodyguard)\add*, and David’s sons were \add his\add* administrators.
\c 9
\s1 David assists Mephibosheth
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 One day David asked, “Is there still anyone remaining from Saul’s extended family because I’d like to show kindness to them for Jonathan’s sake?”
\p
\v 2 Now there was a servant in Saul household named Tsiva and he was summoned to David, and the king asked him, “Are you Tsiva?”
\p “\add Yes, I’m\add* your servant,” he replied.
\p
\v 3 “Is there anyone from Saul’s family who’s still alive?” the king asked. “\add If so,\add* I’d like to show God’s kindness to them.”
\p “\add Yes,\add* There’s still one of Jonathan’s sons alive,” Tsiva told the king. “He’s crippled in both legs.”
\p
\v 4 “Where is he?” the king asked.
\p “Actually,” Tsiva told the king, “he’s at Makir’s house—the son of Amiyel in Lo-Devar.”
\v 5 So King David sent messengers to Lo-Devar to have him brought \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\p
\v 6 Then Jonathan’s son Mephibosheth came to David and fell onto his knees and bowed his face down to the ground, and David said, Mephibosheth.”
\p “I’m your servant,” he replied.
\p
\v 7 Then David told him, “Don’t be afraid, because I’ll certainly show you kindness for the sake of your father Jonathan. I’ll restore all the pastureland that belonged to your grandfather Saul, and you yourself are invited to always eat at my table.”
\p
\v 8 Mephibosheth bowed low and asked, “Your servant’s nothing more than a dead dog. Why would you care about me?”
\p
\v 9 Then the king summoned Saul’s servant Tsiva and told him, “I’ve given everything that belonged to Saul and to all his household to your master.
\v 10 You and your fifteen sons and your twenty servants must work the land for him and harvest the produce to support your master’s \add grand\add*son, but he will eat with me at my house.”
\p
\v 11 “\add Yes,\add* my master the king,” Tsiva responded. “Your servant will do everything you’ve commanded.”
\p \add So from then on,\add* Mephibosheth always ate at the king’s table as if he was one of the king’s sons.
\v 12 Mephibosheth had a young son named Mika. All Tsiva’s household became Mephibosheth’s servants,
\v 13 but Mephibosheth (who was crippled in both legs) lived in Jerusalem because he had a permanent invitation to eat with the king.
\c 10
\s1 Israel defeats the Ammonites and the Arameans
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 19:1-19)
\p
\v 1 Some time later, the Ammonite king died, and his son Hanun replace him as king.
\v 2 “I’ll be kind to Nahash’s son Hanun,” David said, “just like his father was kind to me.” So he sent servants \add to Hanun\add* to convey his condolences.
\p When they arrived in the Amon region,
\v 3 the Ammonite commanders said to their master Hanun, “Do you think that David really sent condolences to honour your late father? Isn’t it \add more likely\add* because he wanted them to spy on us, and to explore the city so he could more easily conquer it?”
\p
\v 4 So Hanun seized David’s servants and had half of their beards shaved off and their robes cut in half \add all the way\add* up to their buttocks, then he sent them off.
\v 5 They sent messengers ahead to tell David because they were very humiliated, and the king told them to stay in Jericho before returning when their beards grew back.
\p
\v 6 Then the Ammonite leaders realised that they’d now caused David to despise them, so they hired twenty thousand Aramean \add (or Syrian\add* \add )\add* mercenaries from Beyt-Rehov and Tsovah, and \add another\add* one thousand from Ma’akah and twelve thousand from Tov.
\v 7 When David heard that, he sent Joab in with the entire army.
\v 8 The Ammonites came out the \add city\add* gate and organised themselves for battle at the entrance, while their hired mercenaries stood apart out in the countryside.
\p
\v 9 When Joab saw that he was disadvantaged with enemy lines both in front and behind, he selected certain Israelite warriors and placed them to battle the Aramean mercenaries,
\v 10 leaving the others to face the Ammonites under the command of his brother Avishay,
\v 11 telling him, “If the Arameans are too strong for me, then you come and rescue us, but if the Ammonites are too strong for you, then I’ll come and rescue you.
\v 12 Be strong and courageous for \add the sake of\add* our people and our god’s cities, and Yahweh will do whatever he considers right.”
\p
\v 13 So Joab and his men advanced to attack the Aramean mercenaries but they fled away from them.
\v 14 When the Ammonites saw the Arameans running away, they started retreating from Avishay and went back into the city, so Joab withdrew from attacking the Ammonites and returned to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 15 When the Arameans realised that they’d been defeated by Israel, they assembled \add all their warriors\add* together \add again\add*.
\v 16 \add King\add* Hadadezer \add (from Tsovah)\add* called for Aramean warriors from beyond the \add Euphrates\add* river and they came to Heylam. Shovak was Hadadezer’s army commander.
\v 17 When David heard that, he assembled all the Israelite warriors and they crossed the Jordan going towards Heylam. The Arameans came out to meet David and the two sides fought each other.
\v 18 But \add again\add* the Arameans fled from the Israelites, and David killed seven hundred Aramean charioteers and forty thousand cavalry, and their commander Shovak was struck and died there \add also\add*.
\v 19 Then all the kings associated with Hadadezer realised they couldn’t defeat Israel, so they made peace with the Israelites and served them, and \add so\add* the Arameans were afraid to \add try to\add* rescue the Ammonites again.
\c 11
\s1 David takes Uriah’s wife
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 The next spring (when kings usually go to war), David sent Joab and his officials and all the Israelite warriors, and they defeated the Ammonites and besieged Rabah.
\p But David stayed in Jerusalem,
\v 2 and early one evening he got up from a nap and walked around the palace roof area. From there he saw a woman bathing, and she was very good-looking.
\v 3 David sent \add a messenger\add* to find out who she was, and was told, “That’s Eliam’s daughter Bathsheba—Uriah the Hittite’s wife.”
\v 4 Then he sent messengers to get her, and she came and he slept with her. (She had been purifying herself after the completion of her menstrual period.) Then she returned to her house.
\p
\v 5 After some time, the woman \add realised that she\add* was pregnant, and \add so\add* she informed David.
\v 6 David sent to Joab, “Send Uriah the Hittite \add here\add* to me.” So Joab sent Uriah to David.
\v 7 When he arrived, David asked him about how Joab and his men were doing, and how the battle was going,
\v 8 then he told him, “Go to your house and relax.” So Uriah left the palace and the king sent a gift to be delivered to his house.
\v 9 But Uriah didn’t go home—instead he slept at the palace entrance with all the king’s servants,
\v 10 \add The next day,\add* David was informed that Uriah didn’t go home to his house, so he asked him, “Didn’t you just get back from a long journey? Why didn’t you go home?”
\p
\v 11 “The sacred chest and \add the warriors from\add* Israel and Judah are living in tents,” Uriah replied. “And my master, Joab, and my master’s servants are camping out in the open. So I couldn’t just go to my house to eat and to drink and to sleep with my wife. By your life and by the life of your spirit, I couldn’t do that.”
\p
\v 12 “\add Well,\add* stay here today also,” David told him, “and tomorrow I’ll send you \add back\add*.” So Uriah stayed in Jerusalem that day and the following day,
\v 13 and David summoned him \add over\add*, and they ate and drank together, and David got him drunk. But even then, Uriah didn’t go back to his house, but when he left in the evening he went \add back\add* to sleep \add in the dorm\add* with his master’s servants.
\p
\v 14 The next morning, David wrote a note to Joab and \add told\add* Uriah to deliver it.
\v 15 He’d written, “Place Uriah in the front where the fighting is strongest, then fall back from behind him so that he’ll be struck and die.”
\v 16 So as Joab was surrounding the city, he placed Uriah where he knew the strongest enemy warriors \add would be fighting\add*.
\v 17 The warriors from the city came out and fought against Joab’s men, and some of David’s servants were killed and so was Uriah.
\p
\v 18 Then Joab sent \add a messenger\add* to David to give him a full account of the battle.
\v 19 He told the messenger, “Once you’ve finished telling all the details of the battle to the king,
\v 20 if he gets angry and asks, ‘Why did you all go so close in to the city to fight? Didn’t you know that they’d shoot \add down\add* from the wall?
\v 21 Wasn’t Yerubeshet’s son Avimelek killed by a woman in Tevets when she threw a millstone down from the wall? Why did you approach the wall?’ Then you should say, ‘Your servant Uriah the Hittite is also dead.’ ”
\p
\v 22 So the messenger left and went and told David everything that Joab had told him,
\v 23 saying, “Their men gained ground and came out against us in the countryside, but we forced them back to their city gate.
\v 24 Then their archers were shooting at your men from \add the top of\add* the wall and some of your men were killed, including your servant Uriah the Hittite.”
\p
\v 25 David sent the messenger back to encourage Joab, “Don’t let yourself be upset by what happened because no one knows who might die in a battle. Reinforce your battle against the city and tear it down.”
\p
\v 26 When Uriah’s wife Bathsheba heard that her husband had been killed, she mourned for him.
\v 27 When her time of mourning was over, David sent for her and brought her to the palace. She became a wife to him and bore a son for him, but Yahweh was unhappy about what David had done.
\c 12
\s1 Nathan brings rebuke to David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 So Yahweh sent Nathan to David to tell him, “\add Once\add* there was a rich man and a poor man living in the same city.
\v 2 The rich man had a large number of flocks and herds,
\v 3 but the poor man only had one little female lamb that he’d bought. It grew up with him and his sons, and would eat his left-overs and drink from his cup and lay down in his lap—it was like a daughter to him.
\v 4 \add One day\add* the rich man had a visitor, but instead of taking one of his own animals for a meal, he killed the poor man’s lamb and prepared it for the meal for his guest.”
\p
\v 5 David got very angry listening to that and told Nathan, “By the life of Yahweh, the man that did that definitely deserves to be put to death!
\v 6 He must pay back four lambs for doing that and for having no pity \add on that poor man\add*.”
\p
\v 7 Then Nathan said to David, “You are \add that rich\add* man. Yahweh, the god of Israel, says to you: ‘I myself anointed you as king over Israel, and I myself kept you safe from Saul.
\v 8 I gave you the house that belonged to your master, as well as his wives. I made you king over \add both\add* Israel and Judah. And if that wasn’t enough, I’ve given you more and more.
\v 9 So why have you despised my commands by doing what you knew was evil? You had Uriah the Hittite killed with an Ammonite sword and you took his wife for yourself.
\v 10 So now, because you despised me and took Uriah’s wife as your own, war and violence will never go away from you and your descendants.
\v 11 I will cause a disaster to hit you from within your own household—I’ll take your wives and give them to a family member right in front of your eyes, and he will let everyone know that he’s sleeping with your wives.
\v 12 You did what you did in secret, but I myself will do all this in public before all Israel.’ ”
\p
\v 13 “\add Yes,\add* I’ve sinned against Yahweh,” David responded to Nathan.
\p “Yahweh has taken away your sin,” Nathan replied. “You won’t die.
\v 14 Nevertheless, because you have utterly disrespected Yahweh in this matter, also your new son is certainly going to die.”
\v 15 Then Nathan went home.
\s1 David’s son dies
\s4 Full kingdom
\p Then Yahweh caused the baby (that Uriah’s wife had given birth to) to get sick.
\v 16 David pleaded with Yahweh for the boy’s sake, and he fasted, and he spent the \add entire\add* night lying down on the ground.
\v 17 The influential members of his household got beside him to try to get him up from the ground, but he wouldn’t budge, and he wouldn’t eat anything they brought.
\v 18 Then on the seventh day, the baby died, but David’s servants were afraid to tell him because after seeing his response when the child was sick, they were afraid of what he might do to himself when he found out that his son had died.
\p
\v 19 However, when David noticed his servants whispering together, he realised that the baby must have died, and he asked them, “Did the baby die?”
\p “\add Yes,\add*” they answered, “he passed away.”
\p
\v 20 Then David got up from the ground and washed, and he rubbed lotions on and changed his clothes, then he entered Yahweh’s tent and worshipped him. Then he went back home and asked for food to be served, and he ate.
\p
\v 21 His servants queried him, “We don’t understand what just happened: when the baby was alive you fasted and wept, but yet when he dies, you get up and have a meal?”
\p
\v 22 “While the child was still alive,” David replied, “I fasted and I wept, because I thought, ‘Who knows? Yahweh might be gracious to me and let him live.’
\v 23 But now \add that\add* he’s dead, there’s no more reason to fast—I can’t bring him back again. \add One day\add* I’ll go to him, but he’ll never come back \add here\add* to me.”
\v 24 Then David comforted his wife Bathsheba.
\s1 The birth of Solomon
\s4 Full kingdom
\p Then David went to Bathsheba and slept with her, and \add in due course\add* she gave birth to a son. David he named him Solomon, and Yahweh loved that boy
\v 25 and sent the prophet Nathan to name him Yedideyah\f + \fr 12:25 \ft That name didn’t seem to stick because this is the only mention of it in the Bible.\f* \add (which means ‘loved by Yahweh’)\add* for Yahweh’s sake.
\s1 David captures Rabah
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 20:1-3)
\p
\v 26 Then Joab fought the Ammonites at Rabah and captured their palace,
\v 27 then he sent messengers to David to tell him, “I’ve fought against Rabah and I’ve overthrown the city’s water supply.
\v 28 So now, gather the rest of our fighters and lay siege to the city and capture it. Otherwise I’ll do it and then it’ll be named after me.
\v 29 So David gathered all his warriors and went to Rabah, \add where\add* he attacked the city and captured it.
\v 30 He took the king’s crown off his head (it was gold and weighed around thirty kilograms), and it was placed on his head. A lot of other plunder was also taken from the city.
\v 31 The inhabitants of Rabah were brought out and assigned to work at the brick kiln with saws, iron picks, and axes. (He did that to all the Ammonite cities.) Then David and all his people returned to Jerusalem.
\c 13
\s1 Amnon rapes Tamar
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Some time afterwards, David’s son Amnon fell in love with his beautiful half-sister Tamar (Absalom’s sister),
\v 2 and he made himself frustrated, \add always thinking about\add* her because she was a virgin. However, he couldn’t do anything with her.
\v 3 But Amnon had a very shrewd friend called Yonadav (son of David’s brother Shimeah)
\v 4 and Yonadav asked him, “\add Hey,\add* you’re a son of the king, so tell me why you’re always so unhappy?”
\p “I’m in love with my brother Absalom’s sister Tamar,” Amnon replied.
\p
\v 5 “Go to bed and pretend to be sick,” Yonadav suggested. “Then \add when\add* your father comes to see you, ask him, ‘Please let my sister Tamar come to look after my food. She can cook it here and serve it to me.’ ”
\v 6 So Amnon lay down and pretended to be sick, and \add when\add* the king came to see him, Amnon asked him, “Please, let my sister Tamar come and make a couple of bread rolls while I’m watching, and then she can serve them to me.”
\p
\v 7 So David sent \add a message\add* to Tamar to ask her to go to her brother Amnon’s house and prepare food for him.
\v 8 So Tamar went to her half-brother Amnon’s house where he was lying down. She took some dough and kneaded it into breadrolls and baked them.
\v 9 Then she took the tray over to him, but he refused to eat, saying, “Send everyone else out of here,” so they all left.
\v 10 Then Amnon told Tamar, “Bring the food into the bedroom so that you can serve it to me.” So Tamar took the breadrolls that she’d made, and took them to Amnon in the bedroom.
\v 11 But when she got close enough to him, he grabbed her and insisted, “Come \add and\add* lie down with me, my sister.”
\p
\v 12 “No, my brother,” she told him, “don’t humiliate me because we Israelites don’t do that. Don’t do this disgraceful thing.
\v 13 Where could I hide my shame? And you—you’d be considered a godless fool in Israel. Just speak to the king because he’d give me to you to marry.”
\v 14 But he didn’t want her advice, and being stronger than her, he humiliated her by forcing her down and raping her.
\p
\v 15 Instantly, Amnon hated her with terrible hatred—a much greater hatred than the love with which he’d loved her, and he told her to get up and get out.
\p
\v 16 “No, \add no\add*.” she responded. “Sending me away would be even worse than what you just did to me.”
\p But he refused to listen to her
\v 17 and called his personal servant. He told him to take the woman away and send her outside and then lock the door behind her.
\v 18 (She was wearing the kind of long robe that the virgin daughters of the king wore as an outer garment.)
\p So the servant pushed her outside and locked the door behind her.
\v 19 Then Tamar put ashes on her head and tore the long robe that she was wearing. She put her hand on her head and wailed as she walked \add home\add*.\f + \fr 13:19 \ft The ashes, tearing of clothes, putting hand on head, and wailing, were all cultural signs of mourning and distress.\f*
\v 20 Her brother Absalom asked her, “Did your half-brother Amnon mess with you? But now, my sister, don’t tell anyone since he’s your brother and don’t let it upset you.” So Tamar lived in a state of desolation in her brother Absalom’s house.
\p
\v 21 When King David heard about all that, he got very angry.
\v 22 \add Also,\add* Absalom said nothing to Amnon (either good or bad) because he hated him for humiliating his sister Tamar.
\s1 Absalom kills Amnon
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 23 Two years passed, then Absalom’s shearers were in Baal-Hatsor (near Ephraim and Absalom invited all the king’s sons.
\v 24 Absalom went to the king and asked him, “Listen, please, the shearers are working for your servant. Please, let the king and his servants come with your servant.”
\p
\v 25 “No, my son,” the king replied. “There’s too many of us—it would be too expensive for you.” Absalom he urged him, but he wouldn’t go, however he did bless him.
\p
\v 26 “But if \add you don’t come\add*,” Absalom insisted, “please let my brother Amnon come with us.”
\p “Why should \em he\em* go with you?” the king asked.
\v 27 But Absalom insisted, so the king \add agreed to\add* send Amnon and his other sons.
\v 28 Then Absalom prepared his young men, “Listen now, when Amnon gets a bit drunk from the wine and I tell you all, ‘Strike Amnon!’ then you’ll kill him. You all don’t \add need to\add* be afraid \add because\add* I myself am the one commanding you. Be courageous warriors.”
\v 29 \add At the right time,\add* Absalom’s servants did to Amnon what Absalom commanded them. All the rest of the king’s sons jumped up and onto their mules and fled \add away\add*.
\p
\v 30 While they were still on the way, David heard the news that Absalom had struck all of his sons and killed them, and none were left alive.
\v 31 The king stood up and tore his clothes, then he laid down on the ground. \add Meanwhile\add*, all his servants \add there\add* were tearing their clothes.
\v 32 Then Yonadav (David’s brother Shimeah’s son) spoke up, “My master, don’t imagine that all those young men, the king’s sons, have been murdered. \add I suspect that\add* only Amnon is dead, because Absalom has been determined to do this ever since his sister Tamar was humiliated.
\v 33 So my master, don’t believe the report that all your sons are dead. It’s probably only Amnon who’s dead.
\p
\v 34 \add Meanwhile,\add* Absalom had fled.
\p \add Suddenly\add* the watchman turned and saw, wow, many people coming around the hill along the road behind him.
\v 35 “Look,” Yonadav told the king. “The king’s sons are coming! It’s just what I said.”
\v 36 He’d \add hardly\add* finished speaking when, wow, the king’s sons came in. Then they all cried loudly, and the king and his servants joined them in their wailing.
\p
\v 37 But Absalom \add had\add* fled, and he went to Geshur to stay with King Amihud’s son, Talmay. But David mourned for his son every day.
\v 38 After Absalom fled to Geshur, he stayed there for three years.
\v 39 \add By then,\add* King David was no longer grieving over Amnon’s death and longed to go to \add see\add* Absalom.
\c 14
\s1 Absalom returns to Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now Joab Tseruyah’s son) realised that the king missed Absalom,
\v 2 so he sent for a wise woman from Tekoa and told her, “Now, please put on mourning clothes and don’t put on any make-up. Act like a woman who has been mourning for the dead for several days,
\v 3 and go to the king and give him this message.” Then Joab told her what to say to the king.
\p
\v 4 So the Tekoei woman went to the king and fell onto her knees with his face down to the ground, saying, “Your majesty, help \add me\add*.”
\p
\v 5 “What’s the matter?” the king asked.
\p “Alas, I’m a widow,” she replied. “My husband has died.
\v 6 Your female servant had two sons, and the two of them fought \add each other out\add* in the countryside. There wasn’t anyone there who could stop them, and then one son struck the other and killed him.
\v 7 Now listen, the entire clan has turned against your female servant and demanded, ‘Give us the one who killed his brother, and we’ll kill him in exchange for the life of the brother that he killed. Also, it would mean that this evil thing couldn’t be passed on.’ But that would leave me with no descendants to continue my husband’s name.”
\p
\v 8 “Go home,” the king told the woman, “and I’ll make a decision about what to do.”
\p
\v 9 But the Tekoei woman continued, “The blame,\f + \fr 14:9 \ft Presumably for not putting the murderer to death.\f* my master the king, will be on me and my extended family, but the king and his throne will be innocent.”
\p
\v 10 “Anyone who speaks against you,” said the king, “bring them to me, and then they won’t trouble you any more.”
\p
\v 11 “Please,” she insisted, “may the king ask your god Yahweh to prevent the avenger of blood from increasing the tragedy, so my son won’t be destroyed.”
\p “As surely as Yahweh lives,” he vowed, “not a hair from your son’s head will fall to the ground.”
\p
\v 12 Then the woman said, “Please, let your female servant ask my master the king a question.”
\p “Speak,” he replied.
\p
\v 13 So the woman asked, “Why have you acted like this against God’s people? When the king says that \add to me\add*, isn’t he convicting himself since he hasn’t brought back \em his own\em* banished son?
\v 14 It’s certain that we’ll \add all\add* die. Water that’s spilt on the ground can’t be gathered together \add again\add* but God’s \add not like that\add*. Rather than taking away life, he devises plans so that the one who was banished can be gathered back in.
\v 15 \add The reason\add* that I’ve come now to tell this to the king my master, is because the people have frightened me. So your servant said \add to herself\add*, ‘I will speak, please, to the king. Perhaps the king will honour the request of his female servant.
\v 16 Maybe the king will listen in order to save his female servant from the man who’s cutting me and my son together \add off\add* from the inheritance given by God.’
\v 17 And your female servant thought, ‘Please, let the word of my master the king become my assurance, because my master the king is like God’s messenger \add when it comes to\add* understanding good and evil. And may Yahweh your god be with you.’ ”
\p
\v 18 “\add Hang on,\add*” the king told the woman. “I want you to answer this question honestly.”
\p “Please, let my master the king speak.” she responded.
\p
\v 19 “Did Joab perhaps have anything to do with all this?” he asked.
\p The woman answered, “By the life of your spirit, my master the king, there’s no avoiding anything that my master the king has said. \add Yes,\add* your servant Joab—he himself commanded me, and he himself told your female servant what I should say.
\v 20 Your servant Joab set this up to try to remedy the situation. But my master is wise like the wisdom of one of God’s messengers to know everything that’s \add going on here\add* on earth.”
\p
\v 21 Then the king \add summoned\add* Joab and told him, “All right then, I’ll sort this out. Now go \add and\add* bring back the young man Absalom.”
\p
\v 22 Joab fell onto his knees and bowed his face to the ground, and blessed the king, then he said, “Today, your servant knows that I have found favour in your eyes, my master the king, since the king is going to do what his servant asked.”
\v 23 Then Joab got up and went to Geshur to get Absalom and bring him \add back\add* to Jerusalem,
\v 24 but the king said, “He can go around to his house, but he’s to stay right away from me.” So Absalom lived in his own house, but wasn’t allowed into the palace.
\s1 Absalom and David reconcile
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 25 Now Absalom was admired as the most handsome man in all Israel—from the top of his head to the bottom of his feet, you wouldn’t be able to find a blemish on his skin.
\v 26 He had his hair cut once a year when it got too heavy on him, and when the hair was weighed using standard weights, it would be over two kilograms.
\v 27 Absalom had three sons and a daughter named Tamar—a \add very\add* attractive woman.
\p
\v 28 Absalom lived in Jerusalem for two years without ever getting to see the king,
\v 29 so he sent a message to Joab about getting an invitation to see the king, but Joab wouldn’t come to him. He sent another message, but Joab \add still\add* wouldn’t come,
\v 30 so he instructed his servants, “Listen, Joab’s piece of land with a barley crop is nearby. Go and set fire to it.” So Absalom’s servants burnt Joab’s barley.\f + \fr 14:30 \ft TC: The Septuagint translation adds the following words here: “And Absalom’s servants burnt them up. Then Joab’s servants came to him, tearing their garments. They said…”\f*
\p
\v 31 Then Joab went to Absalom’s house and demanded, “Why did your servants destroy my barley crop?”
\p
\v 32 “Listen, I tried to contact you,” Absalom replied. “I asked you to come here so that I could send you to the king to ask what the point was of bringing me here from Geshur \add if I can’t see him\add*—I might as well have stayed back there. So now, help me to get invited to see the king, and then if I’m considered guilty of a crime, then let him kill me.”
\p
\v 33 So Joab went and informed the king, and he called for Absalom. When he entered, he knelt and bowed his face to the ground in front of the king, and then the king kissed him.
\c 15
\s1 Absalom plans his rebellion
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Sometime after all that, Absalom acquired a chariot and horses and \add hired\add* fifty men to run ahead of him.
\v 2 He would get up early and stand on the side of the road going in and out of the city. Every time that someone came into the city to take a dispute to be settled by the king, Absalom would ask them where they were from, and they’d reply with their city and tribe,
\v 3 then he’d say, “Listen, \add No doubt\add* what you’re saying is sensible and right, but the king hasn’t appointed anyone to listen to people from your area.”
\v 4 Then Absalom would add, “If I was appointed as judge, then every person with a dispute or case could come to me and obtain justice.”
\v 5 Whenever anyone approached him to bow respectfully to him, he would put out his hand and embrace them and kiss them.
\v 6 Absalom acted that way towards every Israelite who came for a dispute to be settled by the king, and \add in doing that\add* he stole the hearts of the Israelite people.
\p
\v 7 Four\f + \fr 15:7 \ft The Hebrew says forty years, but this is interpreted here as a scribal error. (David’s entire reign was only forty years, see 1 Kings 2:11.)\f* years later, Absalom begged the king, “Please, release me to go and fulfill the vow that I made to Yahweh in Hebron,
\v 8 because when I was living in Geshur (in Syria I promised Yahweh that if he brought me \add back\add* to Jerusalem, then I’d worship him \add in Hebron\add*.”
\p
\v 9 “Go in peace,” the king responded, so Absalom left for Hebron.
\v 10 \add From there,\add* he sent sleeper groups into all \add areas\add* of Israel, telling them, “When you all hear the trumpet blasts, then shout, ‘Absalom has become king from Hebron!’ ”
\v 11 Absalom had invited two hundred men from Jerusalem to go with him \add to Hebron\add*, but they were innocent and didn’t know what was about to happen.
\v 12 Then \add while\add* he was offering sacrifices, Absalom \add also\add* sent for David’s adviser Ahitofel from Giloh. By now the conspiracy was strengthening, and Absalom was becoming popular with more and more people.
\s1 The fleeing of David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 13 \add Eventually,\add* someone went and told David, “The loyalty of the Israelite people is turning towards Absalom.”
\p
\v 14 So David told his servants, “Pack up quickly and let’s get out of here, or else we won’t be able to escape from Absalom. Be fast in case he’s heading here soon and overtakes us. That would be a massacre and the whole city would die from their swords.”
\p
\v 15 “Your servants \add here\add* will do everything you tell us,” they replied to the king.
\v 16 So the king left ten of his slave-wives to look after the palace, but he left with all his family.
\p
\v 17 So the king departed with all his people, and they stopped at the last house.
\v 18 Then all his servants passed beside him to go ahead, along with his bodyguards (the Kereti and the Peletim) and six hundred Gitim (from Gath).
\v 19 Then the king said to Itay the Giti, “You don’t have to come with us. You’re a foreigner and an exile from your place, so you could go back and stay with the \add new\add* king.
\v 20 You haven’t been here long, and we don’t even know where we’re going. I certainly need to leave but you can return with your relatives. \add May Yahweh show\add* kindness and faithfulness \add to you all\add*.”
\p
\v 21 “As Yahweh lives, and as my master the king lives,” Itay answered the king, “wherever my master the king goes, your servant will certainly be there \add also\add*, whether it means life or death.”
\p
\v 22 “\add Okay,\add* go ahead then,” David told Itay. So Itay and all his men and their families went with David.
\v 23 All the people along the road cried when they saw them walking by. The king and all the others crossed the Kidron riverbed and went \add up the hill\add* toward the wilderness.
\p
\v 24 What a sight! Even Zadok and all the Levites with him were carrying the box with God’s agreement \add in it\add*, and when they put it down \add again\add*, \add the priest\add* Abiathar caught up \add to them and waited\add* until all the people had finished \add exiting\add* the city.
\v 25 But the king told Zadok, “Take the sacred chest back into the city. If Yahweh favours me, then he’ll help me return, and he’ll let me see it and his place of residence \add again\add*.
\v 26 But if Yahweh says that he isn’t pleased with me, then he’ll do to me whatever he thinks best.
\v 27 Aren’t you a prophet? Return to the city in peace, along with your son Ahima’ats and Abiathar’s son Jonathan.
\v 28 Listen, I’ll wait at the fords in the wilderness until I get your message.”
\v 29 So Zadok and Abiathar carried the sacred chest back to Jerusalem, then they remained there.
\p
\v 30 As David was going up the \add Mt.\add* of Olives, he was walking barefoot with his head covered. He was weeping as were the people with him—they \add too\add* had their heads covered and were weeping.
\v 31 David knew that Ahitofel was among the people conspiring with Absalom, so he prayed, “Please, Yahweh, make Ahitofel give foolish advice.”
\p
\v 32 When David reached the summit (where people \add frequently\add* worship God), Hushay (the Arki) met him there. He had torn his clothes and put dirt on his head.
\v 33 David told him, “If you come with me, you’ll be a burden to me,
\v 34 but if you return to the city and tell Absalom, ‘I’m your servant, my king. I was your father’s servant in the past, but now I’ll be your servant,’ then you’ll \add be able to oppose\add* Ahitofel’s counsel for me.
\v 35 The priests Zadok and Abiathar are already there, so tell them everything you hear in the palace.
\v 36 \add Also\add* Zadok’s son Ahima’ats and Abiathar’s son Jonathan are there. Tell them everything you hear, then send them to report it to me.
\p
\v 37 So David’s friend Hushay slipped back into Jerusalem just as Absalom was entering the city.
\c 16
\s1 Dishonest Tsiva helps David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David hadn’t gone far past the summit when, wow, he was meet by Mephibosheth’s servant Tsiva with a pair of saddled donkeys loaded with two hundred bread rolls, one hundred cakes of raisins, one hundred \add pieces\add* of fresh fruit, and a leather container of wine.
\v 2 “What’s all that?” the king asked.
\p “The donkeys are for the king’s family to ride,” Tsiva answered, “and the bread and fruits are for the young men to eat, and the wine is for the people to drink who feel faint in the wilderness.”
\p
\v 3 “So where’s your master’s grandson \add Mephibosheth\add*?” asked the king.
\p Ha, he stayed in Jerusalem because he thought that the Israelites might give back control of his grandfather’s kingdom,” Tsiva lied.
\p
\v 4 “Well then,” the king told Tsiva, “everything that belonged to Mephibosheth now belongs to you.”
\p “I’m honoured,” Tsiva responded, “that I’ve found favour in the eyes of my master the king.”
\s1 Shimei curses David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 5 When King David got to Bahurim, wow, a man from Saul’s extended family came out. He was Gera’s son Shimei, and he came out and cursed David.
\v 6 Then he threw stones at David and all his servants, and all the people and warriors on both sides of the king.
\v 7 “Go away, go away, you worthless man of much bloodshed!” Shimei yelled.
\v 8 “\add Now\add* Yahweh’s returning to you all the blood of Saul’s household. You took over as king in his place, but Yahweh’s giving the kingdom into the hand of your son Absalom. Look at you, you’re evil because you’re a man of much bloodshed.”
\p
\v 9 The Tseruyah’s son Avishay asked the king, “Why should this dead dog curse my master the king? Please, let me cross over and lop off his head.”
\p
\v 10 “What does it matter to me, or to you \add two\add* sons of Tseruyah?” the king replied. “He’s cursing because Yahweh told him to curse me, so who has any right to ask him why he’s saying it?”
\v 11 Then David told Avishay and all his servants, “Look, my own biological son is trying to kill me. Now on top of that, this Benyamite. Leave him and let him curse, because Yahweh told him to.
\v 12 Perhaps Yahweh will see my suffering and return good on me, instead of this cursing today.”
\v 13 As David and his men continued along the road, Shimei walked along the hillside beside him—walking and cursing, and throwing stones and dirt at him.
\v 14 \add Later\add*, the king and all the people with him arrived,\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Probably at the fords mentioned in 15:28.\f* and feeling very tired, they rested.
\s1 The conflicting advice of Hushay and Ahitofel
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 15 \add Meanwhile,\add* Absalom and all his followers arrived in Jerusalem, and Ahitofel was among them.
\v 16 When David’s friend Hushay (the Arki) came to Absalom, he greeted him, “May the king live. May the king live.”
\p
\v 17 “Is that how you show kindness to your friend \add David\add*?” Absalom responded. “Why didn’t you go with him?”
\p
\v 18 “No,” Hushay replied. “I’ll serve whoever Yahweh and these people and all the men of Israel have chosen. \add So\add* I’ll stay with you.
\v 19 Besides that, who should I serve? Why shouldn’t I serve my master’s son? Just as I’ve served your father, similarly, I’ll serve you.”
\p
\v 20 Then Absalom \add turned to\add* Ahitofel, “Give us advice. What should we do \add next\add*?”
\p
\v 21 “Go to your father’s slave-wives that he left to guard the house,” Ahitofel replied. “\add Lie with them\add* then all Israel will hear that you’ve made your father stink, and then everyone with you will be encouraged.”
\v 22 So they set up a tent for Absalom on the \add palace\add* roof and he had sex with his father’s slave-wives where everyone would know what was going on.
\p
\v 23 In those days, Ahitofel’s advice was just as good as inquiring directly from God, so David in the past, and now Absalom, accepted what he said.
\c 17
\s1 Hushay argues against Ahitofel’s advice
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Ahitofel asked Absalom, “Please, let me choose twelve thousand men, then let me go and pursue after David tonight.
\v 2 I’ll attack him while they’re tired and lacking energy. Once I panic them, all the people with him will flee and I’ll be able to strike the king by himself.
\v 3 Then I’ll bring all the people back to you, like a reunion. You’ll \add have\add* the man you’re wanting, and all the people will be in peace.”
\v 4 That seemed very sensible to Absalom and all the Israelite elders.
\p
\v 5 But Absalom insisted, “Now, call Hushay the Arki as well, and let’s also listen to his suggestions.”
\v 6 So when Hushay arrived, Absalom asked him, “Ahitofel has suggested so and so. Should we do what he said? If not, what would you recommend?”
\p
\v 7 “This time, Ahitofel’s suggestion isn’t \add such\add* good advice,” Hushay replied.
\v 8 You yourself know your father and his men—\add and do remember\add* that they’re powerful warriors—they’re still furious like a bear in the countryside that’s been robbed of its cubs. And your father is an \add experienced\add* man of war, and he won’t spend the night \add there\add* with the people.
\v 9 Listen, he’s \add probably\add* already hiding in a cavern or some other place. \add Also,\add* if some of your men were killed early \add in the fighting\add*, then the rumour would go around that Absalom’s already lost many men.
\v 10 Then even your bravest warriors might lose heart, because all Israel knows that your father is a powerful warrior, and \add also\add* that the men with him are very experienced.
\v 11 So this is what I suggest: Call warriors from all Israel—from Dan \add in the far north\add* to Beersheba \add in the far south\add*. They’ll be as numerous as sand \add grains\add* on the beach and you personally will be able to lead them into battle.
\v 12 Then we’ll be able to find him wherever he is, and attack him like dew blankets the entire area, and neither him nor any of the men with him will survive, not even one.
\v 13 If he escaped into some city, we’d all bring ropes to that city and drag the stones into the valley until even a pebble couldn’t be found there.”
\p
\v 14 Absalom and all the Israelite elders agreed that Hushay’s advice was better than Ahitofel’s. (Yahweh had influenced them to reject Ahitofel’s good advice so he could bring disaster onto Absalom.)
\s1 The warning to David to flee
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 15 Then Hushay told the \add two\add* priests Zadok and Abiathar what Ahitofel had suggested to Absalom and the Israelite elders and what he’d countered it with.
\v 16 Then he told them, “Get that message to David quickly. \add Tell him\add* not to overnight at the fords in the wilderness, but to cross over otherwise he’ll be killed along with everyone else with him.”
\p
\v 17 \add Their sons\add* Jonathan and Ahima’ats were waiting at Eyn-Rogel where a female servant would come and pass the message onto them, because it wouldn’t be safe for them to be seen entering the city. Then they themselves would go and inform King David.
\v 18 But a young man saw them and informed Absalom, but \add meanwhile\add* the two of them went quickly and got to the house of a man in Bahurim where they went down into the well in his courtyard.
\v 19 The woman \add there\add* stretched a covering over the top of the well, and then spread grain over it \add to dry\add*, so it wasn’t obvious.
\v 20 \add Some of\add* Absalom’s servants arrived at the house and asked the woman, “Where’s Ahima’ats and Jonathan?”
\p “They crossed over the creek,” she replied.
\p So they continued searching for them, but gave up \add after a while\add* and returned to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 21 After they’d gone, the two men climbed out of the well, and went \add on\add* and informed King David, “Pack up and quickly cross over the river, because Ahitofel suggested attacking you all immediately.”
\v 22 So David and everyone with him moved on and crossed the Jordan in the night, and by dawn there were all across.
\p
\v 23 When Ahitofel saw that his advice hadn’t been taken, he saddled his donkey and rode to his city. He gave instructions to his household, then he hanged himself. His body was buried in his father’s tomb.
\p
\v 24 \add Meanwhile,\add* David arrived at Mahanayim, but Absalom and all his men had crossed over the Jordan.
\v 25 As the replacement for Joab as army commander, Absalom had appointed Amasa. (He was Yeter’s son, and his mother was Nahash’s daughter Abigail who was the sister of Joab’s mother Tseruyah.)
\v 26 Absalom and his men camped in the Gilead region.
\p
\v 27 While David was in Mahanayim, Shovi (Nahash’s son from Rabah), Makir (Amiyel’s son from Lo-Devar), and Barzilay (the Giladi from Roglim)
\v 28 \add brought\add* bedding and basins, clay pots, wheat and barley, flour and roasted grain, beans and lentils,
\v 29 honey and yogurt, sheep and cheese for David and the people with him to eat because they knew that they’d be hungry and tired and thirsty \add there\add* in the wilderness.
\c 18
\s1 Absalom’s defeat and death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David divided his warriors into units and appointed commanders of hundreds and of thousands.
\v 2 He sent them out in three groups under Joab, Avishay (son of Joab’s brother Tseruyah) and Itay (the Giti), then promised that he’d also join them in battle.
\p
\v 3 But the people complained, “\add No,\add* don’t go out, because if we have to flee, they won’t be worried about us. Even if half of us die, they won’t be worried about us, because you count more to them than ten thousand of us. So it’s better that you stay to help us from the city.”
\p
\v 4 “I’ll do whatever seems best to you,” the king responded. So the king stayed beside the \add city\add* gate while the men went out by their hundreds and thousands.
\v 5 Then he commanded Joab and Avishay and Itay, “For my sake, don’t harm my son Absalom.” All the people heard the king give that command about Absalom to all the leaders.
\p
\v 6 So David’s people went out to the countryside to meet \add the rest of\add* Israel. The battle was in the Ephraim forest
\v 7 and David’s men defeated the rest—twenty thousand men died that day,
\v 8 but as the battle had spread over the entire area, the forest killed more people that day than the sword.
\p
\v 9 Absalom happened to meet some of David’s men. He was riding his mule, and when \add it\add* darted under the think branches of a large terebinth \add tree\add*, his head got caught in the branches and he was left hanging in the air as the mule kept going.
\v 10 Someone noticed him and informed Joab that he’d seen Absalom hanging in a tree.
\p
\v 11 “What!. You saw him but didn’t strike him down?” Joab challenged. “I would have given you ten silver \add coins\add* and a \add leather\add* belt.”
\p
\v 12 “Even if you’d placed one thousand silver coins in my hands,” he replied, “I wouldn’t have harmed the king’s son, because we heard the king’s command with our own ears about looking out for Absalom.
\v 13 Even if I’d ignored my good sense, the king would have found out about it, then even you wouldn’t have stood up for me.”
\p
\v 14 “I’m not going to waste time arguing with you,” Joab responded, then he took three spears \add and went\add* and thrust them into Absalom’s abdomen while he was still alive and dangling from the tree.
\v 15 Then ten young men who carried Joab’s weapons surrounded Absalom and finished him off.
\p
\v 16 Then Joab blew the trumpet to signal the end of the fighting and the people returned from chasing Absalom’s men.
\v 17 They took Absalom’s \add body\add* and threw it into a large pit in the forest and covered it with a large pile of stones. \add Meanwhile\add* his men fled back to their homes.
\p
\v 18 During his lifetime, Absalom had setup a pillar in the King’s Valley because he had no sons to preserve his name. He put his name on the pillar and it’s known as ‘Absalom’s hand’ to this day.
\s1 David learns about Absalom’s death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 19 Then Zadok’s son Ahima’ats requested, “Please, let me run and let me take the news to the king, because Yahweh has rescued him from his enemies.”
\p
\v 20 “No, not today,” Joab replied. “Some other time I’ll allow you to take some news, but not today because the king’s son is dead.”
\v 21 Then Joab instructed a Kushi man, “Go and tell the king what you saw.”. The man bowed to Joab and ran \add off\add*.
\p
\v 22 But Ahima’ats begged Joab again, “Regardless of what might happen, please, let me also run myself after the Kushi.”
\p “Why would you do that, my boy?” Joab asked. “There won’t be any reward for \add bad\add* news.”
\p
\v 23 “No matter what, I want to run,” \add he replied\add*.
\p “\add Okay then,\add* run,” Joab told him. So Ahima’ats took a different route through the Jordan valley and arrived before the Kushi.
\p
\v 24 Now David was sitting between the outer and inner gates. When the watchman had gone over the gate roof to the wall, he looked out and saw, wow, a man was running \add towards them\add* alone.
\v 25 The watchman called \add down\add* and informed the king, and the king said, “If he’s alone, he’ll be bringing news.”
\p As the runner got closer,
\v 26 the watchman looked and saw another man running. He called \add down\add* to the gatekeeper and said, “Look, there’s \add another\add* man running alone.”
\p “He’ll also be bringing news,” said the king.
\p
\v 27 “The gait of the first runner is like that of Zadok’s son Ahima’ats,” the watchman said.
\p “He’s a good man and he’ll be bringing good news,” said the king.
\p
\v 28 Then Ahima’ats called \add ahead\add* to the king, “Peace!” And he knelt down with his face to the ground and said, “Blessed be Yahweh your god, who’s stopped the men who acted against my master the king.”
\p
\v 29 “Is the young man Absalom all right?” the king asked.
\p “When Joab sent me, your servant,” Ahima’ats answered, “I noticed a big commotion, but I don’t know what \add it was about\add*.”
\p
\v 30 “Go and stand over there,” the king ordered, so he stepped aside and stood \add there\add*.
\p
\v 31 Then, wow, the Kushi \add man\add* arrived and said, “May my master the king receive the good news, because Yahweh has rescued you today from all those who rose up against you.”
\p
\v 32 “Is the young man Absalom all right?” the king asked him.
\p The man replied, “May the enemies of my master the king and all those who rose up against you for evil, be like that young man \add is\add*.”
\p
\v 33 Then the king \add started\add* trembling, and he went up to the room over the gate, and \add started\add* weeping, saying, “Oh my son Absalom, my son. Oh my son Absalom, I wish I’d died instead of you.”
\c 19
\s1 Joab scolds David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Soon\add* Joab was told that the king was weeping and mourning over Absalom’s \add death\add*,
\v 2 and \add so\add* the victory of that day became mourning for all the people, because they’d heard that the king was grieving for his son.
\v 3 The people \add started\add* quietly heading back into the city, like people would do when they’re ashamed of having to \em flee\em* from a battle.
\v 4 But the king covered his face and \add kept\add* crying out loudly, “Oh my son Absalom. Oh Absalom, my son, my son.”
\p
\v 5 Then Joab went to the house where the king was and said, “Today you’ve made your followers feel ashamed—the ones who saved your life today and the lives of your sons and daughters, and the lives of your wives and your slave-wives.
\v 6 By loving those who hate you and hating those who love you, you’ve \add effectively\add* declared today that your officers and men mean nothing to you. \add It seems to us that\add* if Absalom was still alive today and we were all dead, then it would have all been okay to you.
\v 7 So get up now and go and speak sincerely to your servants, because as Yahweh lives, if you don’t then you won’t have a single warrior still with you by the end of the night, and \add that would result in\add* more trouble for you than anything else that’s happened to you since you were \add just\add* a lad.”
\v 8 So the king got up and \add went and\add* sat at the \add city\add* gate , and the news quickly got around and all the people came to support the king.
\s1 David returns to Jerusalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p But Absalom’s warriors had all returned to their homes
\v 9 and over time, people all across Israel began to quarrel and say, “The king rescued us from the plans of our enemies including the Philistines, but now he’s left Israel and fled away from Absalom.
\v 10 We anointed Absalom to be over us, but he died in the battle. So why aren’t we doing something about returning \add David as\add* king?”
\p
\v 11 So King David sent the \add two\add* priests Zadok and Abiathar to the leaders of Judah to ask, “Why are you the last group to return the king to the palace since the king has heard that the rest of Israel want it?
\v 12 \add Tell them,\add* ‘You’re all my relatives—my own flesh and blood, so why \add would\add* you be the last to support me \add again\add*?’ ”
\v 13 And tell Amasa, ‘Aren’t you my \add own\add* flesh and bone? May God make me suffer if you don’t become my army commander from now on, to replace Joab.’ ”
\v 14 \add In that way,\add* David united all the people of Judah and they notified the king, “\add We all want\add* you and all your servants to return \add here\add*.”
\p
\v 15 So the king headed back and reached the Jordan \add river\add*, and \add men from\add* Judah went to Gilgal to meet the king and help him across.
\p
\v 16 Shimei (son of Gera the Benyamite from Bahurim) hurried down with the Judah men to meet King David.
\v 17 A thousand Benyamite men came with him, as well as Tsiva (formerly Saul’s servant) and fifteen of his sons and twenty of his servants, and they hurried to the Jordan to meet the king.
\v 18 They crossed the ford \add to help\add* bring the king’s household \add back\add* across and anything else the king wanted.
\s1 David’s mercy to Shimei
\s4 Full kingdom
\p Shimei (Gera’s son) fell onto his knees in front of the king when he crossed the Jordan
\v 19 and begged the king, “May my master not consider me guilty, and don’t keep thinking about the terrible thing your servant dig on the day that my master the king left Jerusalem—don’t brood over it
\v 20 because your servant knows that I myself have sinned, and look, I have come today, first of all Joseph’s clans to come down to meet my master, the king.”
\p
\v 21 But Avishay (Tseruyah’s son) answered \add instead\add*, “Rather than that, shouldn’t Shimei be killed because he cursed Yahweh’s anointed one?”
\p
\v 22 “You sons of Tseruyah!” David responded. “Who asked you \add two\add* to accuse others to me today? Should today be a time to kill other Israelites? Actually, I think it might be \em me\em* who’s Israel’s king?”
\v 23 Then the king promised Shimei, “You won’t die for that.”
\s1 David’s mercy to Mephibosheth
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 24 Then Saul’s grandson Mephibosheth came down to meet the king. He hadn’t shaved or washed his feet, and he hadn’t washed his clothes since the king had left Jerusalem.
\v 25 He came from Jerusalem down to the Jordan to meet the king who asked him, “Why didn’t you not come with me, Mephibosheth?”
\p
\v 26 “My master the king,” Mephibosheth replied, “my servant deceived me. I, your servant, had said to get my donkey saddled so I could ride it and go with you, because \add as you know,\add* your servant is lame.
\v 27 Then my servant lied to you about me. But my master the king is like God’s messenger, so do whatever you consider to be appropriate.
\v 28 All my grandfather’s household expected that we’d be executed, yet to placed me, your servant, among those who eat at your table, so I don’t have the right to request anything further from the king.”
\p
\v 29 “Say no more,” said the king. “That land will be divided for you and Tsiva.”
\p
\v 30 Actually, let him take everything,” Mephibosheth responded. “since my master the king has been able to return safely.”
\s1 David’s kindness to Barzilay
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 31 Barzilay the Giladi had \add also\add* come down from Roglim to help the king cross the Jordan.
\v 32 At eighty, he was very old, but being very wealthy, he’d provided food and supplies to the king while he’d stayed at Mahanayim,
\v 33 so the king told him, “You cross over with me, and I’ll provide for you to be with me in Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 34 “How much longer am I likely to live?” Barzilay pondered. “Would it really be worth me moving to Jerusalem with the king?
\v 35 Your servant is eighty years old. My mind isn’t the same as it was, and I can’t really taste what I eat or drink. I can barely hear the sounds of the men and women singing. So why would your servant become an extra burden to my master the king?”
\v 36 As a token privilege, your servant will cross the Jordan with the king, but why should the king reward me with that \add generous\add* reward?
\v 37 Please, let your servant return, and let me die in my city near the graves of my parents. But look, \add here’s\add* your servant Kimham—let him cross over with my master the king, and do whatever you consider to be appropriate for him.”
\p
\v 38 “Kimham can cross over with me,” the king said, “and I myself will do for him whatever \em you\em* consider to be appropriate—everything that you choose concerning me, I’ll do for you.”
\v 39 So all the people crossed the Jordan, and then the king crossed \add over\add*. The king kissed Barzilay and blessed him, and then Barzilay returned home.
\s1 Judah and Israel disagree
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 40 Then the king crossed over to Gilgal, and Kimham crossed over with him. All the people of Judah brought the king over and also \add about\add* half of the people of Israel.
\v 41 Then, wow, all the men of Israel went to the king and demanded, “Why did our relatives from Judah secret you away \add without inviting us\add*? They took you and your household over the Jordan, and all your men as well.”
\p
\v 42 All the men of Judah answered the men of Israel, “Because the king is \add from our tribe\add*—our close relative. Why should that make you all angry? Do you all think that the king has rewarded us with fancy food or other privileges?”
\p
\v 43 The men of Israel answered the men of Judah, “\add Our ten tribes\add* give us ten shares in the king—more than you. So why do you look down on us? Weren’t we the first to talk about bringing our king back \add to Jerusalem\add*?”
\p But what the men of Judah said was harsher than what the ten other tribes said.
\c 20
\s1 Sheba rebels against David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now there was a good-for-nothing Benyamite man named Sheba who was Bikri’s son, and he blasted on the trumpet, then said,
\q1 “We have no interest in David,
\q1 and no claim in Jesse’s son.
\q1 Go home every man from Israel.”
\p
\v 2 So all the men of Israel left David and went with Sheba (Bikri’s sons), but the men of Judah stayed with their king from the Jordan \add river\add* and uphill to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 3 When David got back to his Jerusalem palace, the ten slave-wives that he’d left behind to guard the palace, he moved them into a \add separate house\add*. \add That place was\add* guarded and he provided for them, but he didn’t sleep with them—thus they were locked up until they died living as widows.
\p
\v 4 \add One day\add* the king told Amasa \add (his new army commander)\add*, “Summon the men of Judah to be here in three days, and you be with them.”
\v 5 So Amasa went to summon them, but he took longer than the given time.
\v 6 \add Meanwhile\add* David told Avishay, “Bikri’s son Sheba will do more harm to us now than Absalom. Take some of my warriors and chase him down, in case he finds some fortified city to hide in and gets away from us.”
\v 7 So Joab’s men and the king’s bodyguards and all the best warriors left Jerusalem to hunt Sheba down.
\v 8 When they were approaching the huge rock at Gibeon, they met Amasa. Joab was dressed for battle with a dagger strapped to his waist under his robe. As he stepped forward, he allowed his sword to drop to the ground \add from his hand\add*.
\v 9 Joab greeted Amasa, “Is all well with you, my brother?” Then he used his right hand to hold Amasa’s beard to kiss him,
\v 10 but Amasa wasn’t aware of the dagger that was in Joab’s \add other\add* hand. Joab thrust it into his stomach and his intestines poured out on the ground. There was no need \add to stab him again\add* and Amasa died \add right there\add*.
\p Then Joab and his brother Avishay \add continued\add* their hunt for Bikri’s son Sheba.
\v 11 One of Joab’s young men stood beside Amasa’s body and called out, “All those who favour Joab and are for David, follow Joab.”
\v 12 Amasa’s bloody body was in the middle of the road, and when the \add young\add* man saw that everyone was stopping to look at it, he dragged it off the road onto the grass and threw a cloth over it.
\v 13 After the body had been removed from the road, all the men followed after Joab to chase Sheba.
\p
\v 14 \add Meanwhile\add* Sheba crossed through all the tribal regions of Israel to Abel-Beyt-Ma’akah where all his relatives gathered together and agreed to back him.
\v 15 Joab’s men arrived and Abel-Beyt-Ma’akah. They heaped a siege ramp up against the city, and \add also\add* pounded the wall to \add try to make it\add* collapse.
\v 16 Then a wise woman called out from the city, “Listen, listen, please tell Joab to come closer so I can speak to him.”
\v 17 Joab went in closer and she asked, “Are you Joab?”
\p “I am,” he replied.
\p “Listen to what your servant has to say,” she said.
\p “I’m listening,” he answered.
\p
\v 18 Then she told him, “Long ago people used to tell others to come here to Abel to get advice, and they’d do that and then be able to settle the matter.
\v 19 I’m a peaceable and faithful citizen of Israel. You’re trying to destroy this city and a mother in Israel. Why would you wipe out our inheritance from Yahweh?”
\p
\v 20 “Far be it, far be it for me that I would destroy or wipe it out,” Joab replied.
\v 21 That’s not what we’re here for. \add We’re here\add* for a man from the Ephraim hills named Sheba, the son of Bikri. He took a stand against the king—against David. \add Just\add* hand him over to us, and then we’ll leave your city.”
\p “Wait,” the woman told Joab. “His head will be thrown over the wall to you.”
\v 22 So the woman went to all the people with her wisdom, and they decapitated Sheba, and threw his head \add down\add* to Joab. Then he signalled on the trumpet, and his men left the area and went back to their homes, and Joab returned to the king at Jerusalem.
\s1 David’s officials
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 23 Now Joab was over the entire Israelite army, and Benayah (Yehoyada’s son) was over the kings bodyguards.
\v 24 Adoniram\f + \fr 20:24 \ft ‘Adoram’ is a shortened form of his name.\f* was over the forced labour, and Ahilud’s son Jehoshaphat was the secretary and
\v 25 Sheva was the scribe. Zadok and Abiathar were the priests,
\v 26 and Ira (the Yairi) was David’s priest.
\c 21
\s1 The Givonim claim on Saul’s descendants
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 During David’s reign, there was a three-year famine. David inquired from Yahweh who said, “Saul and his family have blood on their hands, because he killed the Givonim.”
\v 2 (The Givonim were not native Israelites, but were a remnant of the Amorites. The Israelites had promised to protect them, but Saul had attempted to eliminate them in his zeal for the people of Israel and Judah.) So the king summoned the Givonim and asked them,
\v 3 “What can I do for all of you? How can I remedy \add what was done in the past\add* so that you all would bless Yahweh’s people?”
\p
\v 4 “We have no claim to gold or silver from Saul or his household,” the Givonim replied, “And we don’t wish to have anyone in Israel put to death.”
\p “Then what are you saying that I can do for you all?” he asked.
\p
\v 5 “That man who crushed us,” they replied, “and who intended that we be annihilated from within Israel’s borders,
\v 6 give us seven of his descendants and we’ll hang them before Yahweh in Giveah where Yahweh’s chosen king Saul lived.
\p “\add Okay\add*, I’ll give them to you,” the king responded.
\p
\v 7 However, the king spared Jonathan’s son (and Saul’s grandson) Mephibosheth because of the promises that he and Jonathan had made to each other before Yahweh.
\v 8 Instead he took two of the sons (Armoni and Mephibosheth) of Ritspah (Ayah’s daughter and one of Saul’s slave-wives), as well as five sons of Saul’s daughter Michal, that she’d bore to Adriel (the son of Barzilay, the Meholati).
\v 9 He handed them over to the Givonim, and they hung them on a hill before Yahweh, and the seven of them died together. That happened at the beginning of the barley harvest.
\p
\v 10 Then Ayah’s daughter Ritspah took \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth and she spread it on the rock \add where the corpses were\add*. She kept the birds away during the day and the animals at night—staying there from the beginning of the harvest until the beginning of the rainy season.
\p
\v 11 David was told what Ritspah had done,
\v 12 and he went and got the bones of Saul and his son Jonathan from the leaders of Yavesh-Gilead, who had stolen them from the square of Beyt-Shean where the Philistines had hung them there on the day the Philistines had defeated Saul at Gilboa.
\v 13 David took Saul and Jonathan’s bones along with the bones of the \add seven\add* men who’d been hung,
\v 14 and his men buried them in the tomb of Saul’s father Kish in Tsela (in the Benyamite region), doing everything that the king had commanded. After that, God answered their prayers for the country.
\s1 Battles against Philistine giants
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 20:4-8)
\p
\v 15 The Philistines started attacking Israel again, and David and his men went down \add towards the coast\add* and fought back, but David became exhausted.
\v 16 \add The Philistines had a champion\add* named Yishbi-Benov who was a descendant of giants. He wore new \add armour\add* and his bronze spear weighed over three kilograms. He intended to kill David
\v 17 but Tseruyah’s son Avishay helped David, and he struck the Philistine and killed him. Then, David’s men made a decision, saying, “You won’t go out to battle with us anymore because we don’t want Israel’s lamp to be extinguished.”
\p
\v 18 \add Some time\add* after that, there was another battle with the Philistines at Gov, and Sibkay (the Hushati) killed Saf who was another descendant of the giants.
\p
\v 19 Then in \add another\add* battle with the Philistines at Gov, Elhanan (son of Ya’are-Oregim, from Bethlehem killed Goliath the Giti even though his spear handle was like a weaver’s beam.
\p
\v 20 In a different battle in Gath, there was a fierce man there with six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot who was also a descendant of the giants,
\v 21 but when he taunted Israel, Jonathan son of David’s brother Shimeah) killed him.
\v 22 Those four men were descendants of the giants in Gath, but they were killed by David and his men.
\c 22
\s1 David’s song of praise
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (Psa. 18)
\p
\v 1 Then after Yahweh had rescued David from Saul and all his enemies, David sang this song for Yahweh,
\q1
\v 2 “Yahweh is my rock and my fortress
\q1 and the one who rescues me, \add yes,\add* even me.
\q1
\v 3 God of my rock—I find safety in him,
\q1 my shield and my powerful saviour,
\q1 my high fort and my refuge,
\q1 my saviour—you save me from violence.
\q1
\v 4 I call out to Yahweh who \add deserves\add* to be praised.
\q1 I was kept safe from my enemies.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Death was enveloping me like a wave.
\q1 Torrents of worthlessness were overwhelming me.
\q1
\v 6 Sheol’s ropes tightened around me.
\q1 Deadly traps confronted me.
\q1
\v 7 In my distress I called to Yahweh,
\q1 and to my god I called out.
\q1 He listened to my voice from his temple,
\q1 and he heard my cry for help.
\b
\q1
\v 8 The earth reeled and it shook.
\q1 The foundations of the heavens trembled,
\q2 and they shook because he became angry.
\q1
\v 9 Smoke went up from his nose,
\q1 and burning coals and fire from his mouth.
\q1
\v 10 He stretched out the heavens,
\q1 then he came down and it was dark below him.
\q1
\v 11 He rode on a winged creature, and he flew.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* he was seen on the wings of the wind.
\q1
\v 12 He placed darkness all around him as a covering—
\q1 a collection of dark clouds holding water in them.
\q1
\v 13 From the brightness in front of him,
\q2 coals of fire burnt.
\b
\q1
\v 14 Yahweh thundered from the sky.
\q1 The most high God made his voice known.
\q1
\v 15 He sent out arrows, and he scattered them.
\q1 He sent out lightning, and he confused them.
\q1
\v 16 The channels of the sea became visible.
\q1 The foundations of the world were uncovered
\q2 at Yahweh’s rebuke,
\q2 from the blast from his nostrils.
\q1
\v 17 He reached down from above and picked me up.
\q1 He pulled me up out of the surging waters.
\q1
\v 18 He rescued me from my powerful enemy—
\q2 from the people who hated me—
\q1 because they were stronger than me.
\q1
\v 19 They confronted me when I was experiencing difficulties,
\q2 ^but Yahweh supported me.
\q1
\v 20 He brought me out into a wide area.
\q1 He rescued me because he was pleased with me.
\b
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh has rewarded me because of my obedience.
\q1 He’s repaid me because my hands were clean,
\q1
\v 22 because I’ve followed Yahweh’s paths,
\q1 and I haven’t acted wickedly \add by turning\add* away from my god.
\q1
\v 23 I’m aware of all his regulations,
\q1 and I haven’t turned away from his decrees.
\q1
\v 24 I’ve done nothing that he can accuse me of,
\q1 and I’ve stopped myself when I wanted to disobey.
\q1
\v 25 Yahweh rewarded me for my obedience,
\q1 because he saw my innocence.
\b
\q1
\v 26 With a faithful person, you show yourself faithful.
\q1 with a blameless warrior, you prove yourself blameless.
\q1
\v 27 With \add someone\add* who purifies themself, you show yourself pure,
\q1 ^but you show yourself deceptive to a crooked person.
\q1
\v 28 You save afflicted people,
\q1 ^but you dislike the proud—you bring \add them\add* down.
\b
\q1
\v 29 Because you are my lamp, Yahweh,
\q1 and you lighten my darkness.
\q1
\v 30 Because by you, I can charge against a troop of warriors.
\q1 With my god’s \add help\add* I can leap over a \add city\add* wall.
\b
\q1
\v 31 God’s path is perfect.
\q1 Yahweh’s message is tried and tested.
\q1 →He keeps everyone safe who requests his protection.
\q1
\v 32 Who could be God other than Yahweh?
\q1 And who is a rock apart from our god?
\q1
\v 33 This god is my strong fortress,
\q1 and he keeps my pathways cleared.
\q1
\v 34 The one who makes my feet \add nimble\add* like a deer,
\q1 and places me securely up on the heights.
\q1
\v 35 The one who trains me how to win in battle,
\q1 and \add strengthens\add* my arms to pull back a powerful bow.
\b
\q1
\v 36 You saved me \add from death\add* like a shield,
\q1 and you helped me become well-known by answering \add my prayers\add*.
\q1
\v 37 You helped me make large strides,
\q1 and stopped my feet from slipping.
\q1
\v 38 Let me chase down my enemies and destroy them.
\q1 Then I won’t return until they’re all defeated.
\q1
\v 39 I will attack them and smash them.
\q1 They won’t stand \add again\add*—they’ll fall under my feet.
\q1
\v 40 You give me extra strength for the war.
\q1 You make the people who rebelled against me submit.
\q1
\v 41 You make my enemies flee away from me.
\q1 \add You help me\add* destroy the people who hate me.
\q1
\v 42 They look, but there’s no one to rescue them.
\q1 \add They call out\add* to you, Yahweh, but you don’t answer them.
\q1
\v 43 I crush them \add as finely\add* as the dust on the ground.
\q1 I trample them like the mud on the streets and flatten them.
\b
\q1
\v 44 You rescue me from the arguments of my people.
\q1 You help me remain as the head of nations.
\q1 People serve me even if I don’t even know them.
\q1
\v 45 Foreigners are afraid of me.
\q1 When they hear me, they obey me.
\q1
\v 46 They lose heart,
\q1 and come \add out\add* of their fortresses trembling.
\b
\q1
\v 47 Yahweh is alive—may my \add secure\add* rock be blessed.
\q1 May the god of the rock of my salvation be praised.
\q1
\v 48 He’s the god who avenges me,
\q1 and the one who makes the people who rebelled against me submit.
\q1
\v 49 He’s the one who helps me escape from my enemies.
\q1 You hold me up \add away\add* from the ones who rise up against me.
\q1 You rescue me from violent men.
\b
\q1
\v 50 Therefore I praise you, Yahweh, among the nations,
\q1 and I sing praises about who you are.
\q1
\v 51 \add The one who\add* helps his king to save \add the people\add*,
\q1 and the one who shows loyalty to the one he chose—
\q1 to David and to his descendants forever.”
\c 23
\s1 David’s ending speech
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 These are David’s final words:
\q1 “The message from Jesse’s son David—
\q1 a message from the man who rose through the ranks—
\q2 who was selected by Jacob’s god—
\q1 \add singer of\add* Israel’s pleasant songs.
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh’s spirit spoke through me.
\q1 His message came out of my mouth.
\q1
\v 3 Israel’s god spoke to me.
\q1 The rock of Israel told me.
\q1 The one who rules people with justice,
\q1 →rules out of his respect for God.
\q1
\v 4 He’s like the rising sun bringing the morning light—
\q2 a cloudless morning.
\q1 Glistening after the rain \add that grows\add* fresh grass from the soil.
\b
\q1
\v 5 My household is aligned with God,
\q1 because he made a perpetual agreement with me—
\q2 all arranged and secured.
\q1 He’ll make my salvation and every desire grow. ???
\q1
\v 6 But evil people are like thorns—
\q2 they’re all thrown away.
\q1 because they injure your hands.
\q2
\v 7 Anyone who \add wants to\add* move them
\q2 must use a metal tool or a wooden stick.
\q1 They’ll be incinerated right there.”
\s1 David’s top warriors
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 11:10-41)
\p
\v 8 These are the names of David’s \add top\add* warriors:
\p \bd Yoshev-Bashevet\bd* the Tahkemoni was the head of ‘The Three’. One time he attacked and killed eight hundred men with his spear.
\p
\v 9 Next after him was \bd Eleazar\bd*, the son of Ahohi’s son Dodo. \add Once he was\add* among the three powerful warriors with David when they taunted the Philistines who were gathered there for battle. The Israelite warriors retreated back up their hill,
\v 10 \add but\add* Eleazar moved forward and attacked the Philistines until his arm became weak, and his hand cramped around his sword. Yahweh achieved a great victory that day, and the other warriors only returned afterwards to plunder \add the dead\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then there was Agge’s son \bd Shama\bd*, the Harari. The Philistines had assembled in formation where there happened to be a field full of lentils. The Israelites fled from the Philistines
\v 12 but Shama remained there in the middle of the field and killed \add many\add* Philistines. So the field was saved, and Yahweh achieved a great victory.
\p
\v 13 \add Once\add* as it was approaching harvest time, three of ‘The Thirty’ went to where David \add had been staying\add* at Adulam’s cave. There was a Philistine unit camped in the Refaim valley.
\v 14 At the time, David was in the fortress, and there was \add another\add* Philistine unit in Bethlehem.
\v 15 David was thirsty \add one day\add* and asked, “Who’ll get me a drink of water from the Bethlehem well by the gate?”
\v 16 So the three powerful warriors broke through the Philistine camp and got water from the Bethlehem well near the gate. They carried it back to David, but he wouldn’t drink it—pouring it out instead \add as an offering\add* to Yahweh,
\v 17 saying, “Far be it for me, Yahweh, to drink this—the blood of the men who risked their lives.” So he wasn’t willing to drink it.
\p Those \add were some of the\add* things done by ‘The Three’ powerful warriors.
\p
\v 18 Joab’s brother \bd Avishay\bd* (Tseruyah’s sons) was the head over ‘The Three’. \add Once\add* he was used his spear to kill three hundred fighters, so he was more famous than ‘The Three’.
\v 19 He was more honoured than ‘The Three’ and became their leader even though he wasn’t one of ‘The Three’.
\p
\v 20 Yehoyada’s son \bd Benayah\bd* from Kavtse’el was a man of military prowess with many actions \add to his name\add*. \add Once\add* he killed ‘The Two’ from Moab, and \add another time\add* he went down in a pit on a snowy day and killed the lion in the pit.
\v 21 He \add also\add* killed an impressive Egyptian warrior with a spear, even though Benayah only held a staff. First he snatched the spear from the Egyptian’s hand, then he killed him with his \add own\add* spear.
\v 22 Those \add were some of the\add* things that Benayah did. He became famous alongside ‘The Three’.
\v 23 (He was more honoured than ‘The Thirty’ but never entered ‘The Three’.) David appointed him over his bodyguard.
\p
\v 24 \add The following\add* were among ‘The Thirty’:
\li1 Joab’s \add other\add* brother Asahel,
\li1 Dodo’s son Elhanan from Bethlehem,
\li1
\v 25 Shamah and Elika the Harodim,
\li1
\v 26 Helets the Palti,
\li1 Ikesh’s son Ira the Tekoei,
\li1
\v 27 Aviezer the Antoti,
\li1 Mevunay the Hushati,
\li1
\v 28 Tsalmon the Ahohi,
\li1 Maharay the Netofati,
\li1
\v 29 Ba’anah’s son Helev the Netofati,
\li1 Rivay’s son Itay from Giveah (in Benjamin),
\li1
\v 30 Benayah the Pireatoni,
\li1 Hiday from the Ga’ash valleys,
\li1
\v 31 Avi-Alvon the Arvati,
\li1 Azmavet the Barhumi,
\li1
\v 32 Elyahba the Sha’alvoni,
\li1 Yashen’s sons,
\li1 Jonathan,
\li1
\v 33 Shamah the Harari,
\li1 Sharar’s son Ahiam the Harari,
\li1
\v 34 Ahasbay’s son Elifelet the Ma’akati,
\li1 Ahitofel’s son Eliam the Giloni,
\li1
\v 35 Hetsro the Karmeli,
\li1 Pa’aray the Arbi,
\li1
\v 36 Nathan’s son Yigal from Tsovah,
\li1 Bani the Gadi,
\li1
\v 37 Tselek the Ammonite,
\li1 Naharay the Be’eroti who carried the weapons for Tseruyah’s son Joab,
\li1
\v 38 the Yitrim Ira and Garev,
\li1
\v 39 and Uriah the Hittite.
\p \add There were\add* thirty-seven \add of them\add* in total.
\c 24
\s1 David takes a census
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 21:1-27)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh was angry against Israel again and he incited David to cause problems for them by saying, “Go \add and\add* order a census of Israel and Judah.”
\v 2 So the king told Joab, the commander of his army, who was with him \add at the time\add*, “Travel throughout all the \add regions of\add* the Israelite tribes, from Dan \add (in the far north)\add* down to Beersheba \add in the far south)\add*, and count the people so that I’ll know the number of fighting men.”
\p
\v 3 “May your god Yahweh multiply the people a hundred times \add over\add*,” Joab responded, “add may my master the king see that happen, but why would my master the king want to do that?”
\v 4 However, the king insisted, so even though Joab and the army commanders \add disagreed\add*, at the king’s command they set off to count the Israelite people.
\p
\v 5 They crossed the Jordan \add river\add* and camped in Aroer, south of the city in the middle of the Gad valley, then \add proceeded\add* to Yazer.
\v 6 Then they went \add north\add* to Gilead and Tahtim-Hodshi, \add before\add* coming to Dan-Yaan and around to Sidon.
\v 7 Then they came to the Tyre fortress and all the cities of the Hivim and the Canaanites before going \add east\add* to Beersheba in the Negev \add wilderness\add* (part of Judah).
\v 8 So they travelled throughout the land for nine months and twenty days before returning to Jerusalem.
\v 9 Then Joab reported the census results to the king: 800,000 fighting swordsmen in Israel, and 500,000 in Judah.
\p
\v 10 However, David had a guilty conscience after he’d had the people counted and he told Yahweh, “I’ve disobeyed you badly, but now, Yahweh, please take away the iniquity of your servant because I’ve been very foolish.”
\p
\v 11 \add When\add* David got up in the morning, Yahweh gave the prophet Gad \add this\add* message
\v 12 to take to David, “I, Yahweh, am offering you three choices. You decide which one I should carry out against you.”
\v 13 Then Gad asked him, “Do you want seven years of famine in your country, or three months of fleeing from your enemies, or three days of deadly disease in your country? Consider those and let me know which option to pass back to the one who sent me.”
\p
\v 14 David answered Gad, “This is very distressing, \add but\add* please let Yahweh be the one to punish me because he’s very merciful—don’t let other men be the ones.”
\p
\v 15 So Yahweh sent a deadly disease to Israel from the morning and until the time he’d decided. Seventy thousand people died from Dan \add in the north\add* down to Beersheba \add in the south\add*.
\v 16 When Yahweh’s messenger stretched out his hand to destroy Jerusalem \add with the deadly disease\add*, Yahweh relented concerning the disaster and told the messenger who was destroying many people, “Now lower your hand.” \add When he said that,\add* his messenger was near the threshing floor of Aravnah the Jebusite.
\p
\v 17 David had complained to Yahweh when he saw the messenger afflicting the people, saying, “Listen, it’s me alone who sinned, and I myself who disobeyed you. But these \add innocent\add* people—what have they done? Please, just punish only me and my relatives.”
\s2 David builds an altar
\p
\v 18 Gad came to David that day, and instructed him, “Go to the Jebusite Aravnah’s threshing floor \add and\add* build an altar to Yahweh \add there\add*.”
\v 19 So David went there just as Yahweh had instructed him through Gad’s message.
\v 20 When Aravnah looked out and saw the king and his servants approaching, he went out and fell to his knees in front of the king and bowed his face to the ground.
\v 21 “Why would my master the king come to his servant?” Aravnah asked.
\p “I want to buy your threshing floor to build an altar for Yahweh,” David answered. “So that the deadly disease afflicting the people can be stopped.”
\p
\v 22 “May my master the king take \add it\add*,” Aravnah responded. “Use it in whatever way you think best. See, \add you can use\add* the oxen for the burnt offering, and the threshing instruments and the oxen’s equipment for firewood.
\v 23 I give \add it all\add* to \add you\add*, the king. May your god Yahweh accept your \add offering\add*.”
\p
\v 24 “No,” the king answered. “I’ll definitely buy it from you. I couldn’t offer something to my god Yahweh \add that cost me\add* nothing.”
\v 25 So David built an altar there to Yahweh, and he instructed for burnt offerings and peace offerings to be made. Then Yahweh accepted prayers for the country and the deadly disease afflicting Israel was stopped.
